window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-24"]=[{"id":"q3-130-134","q":"3:130–134","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:130–134 · Usury, generosity, forbearance, forgiveness and the station of iḥsān","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 406–409","cairo":"I:406–409","source_page_first":406,"source_page_last":409,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَأْكُلُوا الرِّبَوا أَضْعافاً مُضاعَفَةً وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ (130) وَاتَّقُوا النَّارَ الَّتِي أُعِدَّتْ لِلْكافِرِينَ (131) وَأَطِيعُوا اللَّهَ وَالرَّسُولَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُرْحَمُونَ (132) وَسارِعُوا إِلى مَغْفِرَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ وَجَنَّةٍ عَرْضُهَا السَّماواتُ وَالْأَرْضُ أُعِدَّتْ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (133) الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ فِي السَّرَّاءِ وَالضَّرَّاءِ وَالْكاظِمِينَ الْغَيْظَ وَالْعافِينَ عَنِ النَّاسِ وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (134)\nقلت: الكظم هو: الكف والحبس، تقول: كظمت القربة: إذا ملأتها وسددت رأسها.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَأْكُلُوا الرِّبَوا وتزيدوا فيها إذا حلَّ الأجل أَضْعافاً مُضاعَفَةً، ولعل التخصيص بحسب الواقع، إذ كان الرجل يَحُلُّ أجلُ دَيْنِهِ، فيقول للمدين: إما أن تقضي وإما أن تزيد، فلا يزال يؤخره ويزيد في دينه حتى يستغرق مال المدين، فنُهوا عن ذلك. ورغبهم في التقوى التي هي غنى الدارين. فقال: وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ فيما نهيتكم عنه، لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ في الدارين. ثم خوفهم بالنار إن لم ينتهوا، فقال: وَاتَّقُوا النَّارَ الَّتِي أُعِدَّتْ لِلْكافِرِينَ، وفيه إشعار بأن النار موجودة إذ لا يُعدُّ المعدوم، وأنها بالذات معدة للكافرين، وبالعرض للعاصين.\nقال الورتجبي: في الآية إشارة إلى أن النار لم تعد للمؤمنين، ولم تخلق لهم، ولكن خوفهم بها زجراً وعظة، كالأب البار المشفق على ولده يخوفه بالأسد والسيف، وهو لا يضربه بالسيف، ولا يلقيه إلى الأسد، فهذه الآية تلطف وشفقة على عباده. هـ.\nوَأَطِيعُوا اللَّهَ فيما أمر ونهى، وَالرَّسُولَ فيما شرع وسَنَّ، لَعَلَّكُمْ تُرْحَمُونَ. والتعبير بلعل وعسى في أمثال هذه: دليل على عون التوصل إلى ما جعل طريقاً له.\nوَسارِعُوا أي: بادروا إِلى مَغْفِرَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ كالإسلام والتوبة والإخلاص، وسائر الطاعات التي توجب المغفرة، وقرأ نافع وابن عامر بغير واو على الاستئناف. وسارعوا أيضاً إلى جَنَّةٍ عَرْضُهَا السَّماواتُ وَالْأَرْضُ لو وصل بعضها ببعض، وذكر العرض للمبالغة في وصفها بالسعة لأنه دون الطول. قال بعضهم: لم يُرد العَرض الذي هو ضد الطول، وإنما أراد عظمها، ومعناه: كعرض السموات السبع والأرضين السبع في ظنكم، أي: لا تدرك ببيان. أُعِدَّتْ أي: هُيِّئَتْ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ. وفيه دليل على أنَّ الجنة مخلوقة، وأنها خارجة عن هذا العالم.\nثم وصف أهلها من المتقين بأوصاف الكمال، فقال: الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ فِي السَّرَّاءِ وَالضَّرَّاءِ أي: في حالتي الرخاء والشدة، وفي الأحوال كلها، كما هي حالة الأسخياء، قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «الجنَّةُ دَارُ الأسخياءِ» . وقال أيضا:\n«السَّخِيُّ قَرِيبٌ مِنَ اللهِ، قريبٌ مِنَ الجَنَّةِ، قَرِيبٌ من النَّاس، بَعِيدٌ من النَّارِ، والبَخيلُ بَعيدٌ من اللهِ، بَعِيدٌ مِنَ الجَنَّةِ، بَعِيدٌ من الناس، قَرِيبٌ من النَّارِ. ولجَاهلٌ سَخِيٌ أَحَبُ إلى اللهِ مِنَ العالمِ البخيل» . وقال أيضا صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «السّخَاء شَجَرةٌ في الجنة، أغصانها في الدنيا، من تعلق بَغُصنٍ من أغْصَانِها قادَته إلى الجنَّةِ، والبخْلُ شَجرةٌ في النَّارِ، أغصَانُها في الدُّنيا، من تعلق ببعض من أغصانها قَادَته إلى النَّارِ» .\nوَالْكاظِمِينَ الْغَيْظَ أي: الكافين عن إمضائه مع القدرة عليه، قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «من كظم غيظا وهو يَقْدِرُ على إمْضَائِهِ مَلأَ اللهُ قَلْبَهُ أمْناً وإِيمَاناً» .\nوقال بعض الشعراء:\nوَإذَا غَضِبْتَ فَكُنْ وَقُوراً كَاظِماً ... لِلْغَيْظِ، تُبْصر مَا تَقُولُ وتَسْمَعُ\nفَكَفَى بِهِ شَرَفاً، تَصَبُّرُ سَاعَةٍ ... يَرْضَى بِهَا عَنْكَ الإلَهُ ويَرْفَعُ(1)\nوَالْعافِينَ عَنِ النَّاسِ أي: عمن ظلمهم، وعن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال عند ذلك: «إنَّ هؤلاءِ في أُمتي قليل، إلا مَنْ عَصَمَ الله، وقد كَانُوا كثيراً في الأمم التي مَضَت» . وعَنْ أبي هريرة: أن أبا بكر كان مع النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم في مجلس، فجاء رجل فوقع في أبي بكر، وهو ساكت، والنبي صلّى الله عليه وسلم يبتسم، ثم ردّ عليه أبو بكر بعضَ الرد، فغضب عليه الصلاة والسلام- وقام، فلحقه أبو بكر، وقال: يا رسول الله، شتمني وأنت تبتسم، ثم رَدَدْتُ عليه بعضَ ما قال، فغضبتَ وقُمتَ. قال: «حين كنتَ ساكتاً كان معك مَلَكٌ يردُّ عليه، فلما تكلمتَ وقع الشيطان، فلم أكُنْ لأقعدَ في مقعدْ فيه الشيطان، يا أبا بكر، ثلاثٌ حق: تعلم أنه ليس عبد يظلم مظلمة فيعفو عنها إلا أعز الله بها نصره، وليس عبد يفتح باب مسألة يريد بها كثرة إلا زاده الله قلة، وليس عبد يفتح عطية أو صلة إلا زاده الله بها كثرة» .\nوَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ الذي أحسنوا فيما بينهم وبين الله، وفيما بينهم وبين عباد الله، و «أل» : يحتمل أن تكون للجنس، فيعم كل محسن، أو للعهد، فتكون الإشارة إلى من تقدم ذكرهم.\nالإشارة: كل ما يُقوي مادة الحس فهو ربا لأنه يربى الحس ويقوي مادة الغفلة، فلا ينبغي لمريد أن يضاعفه ويتعاطى أسباب تكثيره، بل ينبغي أن يفر من موارده، وهي ثلاثة: مباشرة الحس، أو الفكر فيه، أو الكلام مع أهله\nفيه. والذي يقوي مادة المعنى ثلاثة: صحبة أهل المعنى، والفكرة في المعاني، وذكر الله بالقلب. واتقوا الله فى مباشرة الحس (لعلكم تفلحون) بالوصول إلى صفاء المعاني، واتقوا نار القطيعة التي أعدت لمنكر الخصوصية، (وأطيعوا الله والرسول) فيما ندبكم إليه، (لعلكم ترحمون) بإحياء قلوبكم وأرواحكم بأسرار المعاني، وسارعوا إلى ما يوجب تغطية مساوئكم، حتى يغطي وصفكم بوصفه، ونعتكم بنعته، فيُوصلكم بما منه إليكم، لا بما منكم إليه، فتدخلوا جنة المعارف، التي لا نهاية لفضاء شهودها، التي أعدت للمتقين السّوى، الذين يبذلون مهجهم وأموالهم في حال الجلال والجمال، (والكاظمين الغيظ) حيث ملكوا أنفسهم وأحوالهم، (والعافين عن الناس) لأن الصوفي ماله مباح ودمه هدر. وكان بعض الصوفية يقول: إذا أردتَ أن تعرفَ حال الفقير فأغضبه، وانظر إلى ما يخرج منه. وقال شيخ شيوخنا رضي الله عنه: قطب التصوف: لا تغضب ولا تُغضب. هـ.\nولعروة بن الزبير- رضي الله عنه:\nلن يبلغَ المجدَ أقوامٌ وإنْ كَرُمُوا ... حتى يُذَلّوا وإن عَزّوا لأقوام\nويُشتَموا فتَرى الألوانَ مُشرِقةٌ، ... لا عَفْوَ ذُلَّ، ولكن عَفْوَ أحلام\nوَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ الذين حازوا مقام الإحسان، فعبدوا الله بالشهود والعيان، فعم إحسانهم ذا الإساءة والإحسان والإنس والجان. قال الحسن البصري: (الإحسان: أن يعم إحسانه، ولا يكون كالشمس والريح والمطر) .\nأي: يخص بلداً دون بلد. وقال سفيان الثوري: (ليس الإحسان أن تحسن إلى من أحسن إليك، وإنما الإحسان أن تحسن إلى مَن أساء إليك. فإن الإحسان إلى المحسن متاجرة كنقد السوق، خذ مني وهَات) . وقال السري السقطي:\n(الإحسان: أن تُحسن وقت الإمكان، فليس في كل وقت يمكنك الإحسان) ، وأنشدوا:\nليس في كلَّ ساعةٍ وأوان ... تَتَهَيَّأ صنائعُ الإحسان\nفإذا أمْكَنَتْ فبادرْ إليها ... حذَراً من تعذّر الإمكان(1)\nوقال الورتجبي: قوله: وَسارِعُوا إِلى مَغْفِرَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ وَجَنَّةٍ ... الخ، علم الحق- سبحانه- عِلَلَ الخلق وميلهم إلى مُنى النفوس، فدعاهم بطاعته إلى العلتين: المغفرة والجنة، ودعا الخاصة إلى نفسه، فقال: فَفِرُّوا إِلَى اللَّهِ، ثم أعْلَم أن الكل في درك امتحان الجرم، وأثبت بالآية ذنب الكل، لأنهم وإن كانوا معصومين من الزلل،\nفذنبهم قلة معرفتهم لأقدار الحق، كما قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لو أن الله عذب الملائكة لحق منه، فقيل: إنهم معصومون، فقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: من قلة معرفتهم بربهم(1)» . ولذلك دعاهم إلى المغفرة. هـ. قال في الحاشية: وقوله: (أثبت بالآية ذنب الكل) ، يعني: شمول قوله: (يغفر لمن يشاء) مَنْ فى السموات الصادق بالملائكة، وإنما تكون المغفرة بعد ذنب، ولكنه في كل أحد على حسبه، وأما قوله: دعاهم إلى المغفرة، فكأنه من قوله: سارِعُوا إِلى مَغْفِرَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ، وأن الخطاب يعم من فى السموات أيضاً، وقد يتصور في حق الملائكة الاستنادُ لظواهر الأمور والاختلاف بينهم والاختصام، مما هو معرض للخطأ، وذلك من دواعي المغفرة، وكذلك القصور عن معرفة كنه جلال الله: نقصٌ لا يخلو منه مخلوق، لاستحالة الإحاطة به علماً، ولذلك كان الترقي في المعرفة لا حد له أبداً سرمداً. هـ.\nثم ذكر حال أهل اليمين، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَأْكُلُوا الرِّبَوا أَضْعافاً مُضاعَفَةً وَاتَّقُوا اللَّهَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ (130) وَاتَّقُوا النَّارَ الَّتِي أُعِدَّتْ لِلْكافِرِينَ (131) وَأَطِيعُوا اللَّهَ وَالرَّسُولَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُرْحَمُونَ (132) وَسارِعُوا إِلى مَغْفِرَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ وَجَنَّةٍ عَرْضُهَا السَّماواتُ وَالْأَرْضُ أُعِدَّتْ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (133) الَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ فِي السَّرَّاءِ وَالضَّرَّاءِ وَالْكاظِمِينَ الْغَيْظَ وَالْعافِينَ عَنِ النَّاسِ وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (134)","quran_en":"130. O you who believe, do not consume usury, doubled and redoubled; and be mindful of God, that you may prosper. 131. And guard against the Fire prepared for those who disbelieve. 132. Obey God and the Messenger, that you may receive mercy. 133. Hasten toward forgiveness from your Lord, and a Garden whose breadth is the heavens and the earth, prepared for those who are mindful. 134. Those who give in ease and hardship, restrain their anger and pardon people: God loves those who do what is beautiful (al-muḥsinīn).","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Kaẓm (الكظم) as restraint and containment","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Kaẓm<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكظم<\/bdi>) means restraining and holding back. Thus one says, “I sealed the water-skin” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كظمت القربة<\/bdi>) when one filled it and stopped up its opening.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Kaẓm ( الكظم ) means restraining and holding back. Thus one says, “I sealed the water-skin” ( كظمت القربة ) when one filled it and stopped up its opening."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Redoubled interest and the disposition of God-consciousness","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe, do not consume usury” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرِّبا<\/bdi>) by increasing the debt when its due date arrives, “doubled and redoubled.” Perhaps this specification reflects the customary practice at the time: when a debt fell due, the creditor would tell the debtor, “Either settle it or add to it.” The creditor would grant repeated delays and repeatedly increase what was owing, until the debt swallowed all of the debtor’s property. They were forbidden this. God then commends mindfulness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التقوى<\/bdi>), which is enrichment in both abodes: “Be mindful of God” regarding what I have forbidden you, “that you may prosper” in this world and the next. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N107\">[XIV-N107]<\/a><\/p><p>He then makes them fear the Fire if they do not desist: “Guard against the Fire prepared for the unbelievers.” This also suggests, Ibn ʿAjība argues, that the Fire already exists, since something nonexistent cannot be prepared; that it is prepared for unbelievers primarily (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بالذات<\/bdi>) and for sinful people incidentally (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بالعرض<\/bdi>). Al-Wartajbī says the verse indicates that the Fire was not prepared or created <i>for believers<\/i>, but that God has warned them with it as a deterrent and admonition, just as a kind father may frighten his son with a lion or sword without intending to strike the child with the sword or throw him to the lion. In this interpretation the verse manifests gentleness and care toward His servants. End of quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N108\">[XIV-N108]<\/a><\/p><p>“Obey God” in what He commands and forbids, “and the Messenger” in what he legislates and establishes as Sunnah, “that you may receive mercy.” Forms such as <strong>laʿalla<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لعل<\/bdi>) and <strong>ʿasā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عسى<\/bdi>), “perhaps” or “so that,” in such contexts indicate the assistance granted in reaching the end to which a path has been established.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe, do not consume usury” ( الرِّبا ) by increasing the debt when its due date arrives, “doubled and redoubled.” Perhaps this specification reflects the customary practice at the time: when a debt fell due, the creditor would tell the debtor, “Either settle it or add to it.” The creditor would grant repeated delays and repeatedly increase what was owing, until the debt swallowed all of the debtor’s property. They were forbidden this. God then commends mindfulness ( التقوى ), which is enrichment in both abodes: “Be mindful of God” regarding what I have forbidden you, “that you may prosper” in this world and the next. [XIV-N107] He then makes them fear the Fire if they do not desist: “Guard against the Fire prepared for the unbelievers.” This also suggests, Ibn ʿAjība argues, that the Fire already exists, since something nonexistent cannot be prepared; that it is prepared for unbelievers primarily ( بالذات ) and for sinful people incidentally ( بالعرض ). Al-Wartajbī says the verse indicates that the Fire was not prepared or created for believers , but that God has warned them with it as a deterrent and admonition, just as a kind father may frighten his son with a lion or sword without intending to strike the child with the sword or throw him to the lion. In this interpretation the verse manifests gentleness and care toward His servants. End of quotation. [XIV-N108] “Obey God” in what He commands and forbids, “and the Messenger” in what he legislates and establishes as Sunnah, “that you may receive mercy.” Forms such as laʿalla ( لعل ) and ʿasā ( عسى ), “perhaps” or “so that,” in such contexts indicate the assistance granted in reaching the end to which a path has been established."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The immensity of the Garden","html":"<p>“Hasten” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وسارعوا<\/bdi>), that is, make haste “toward forgiveness from your Lord,” such as Islam, repentance, sincerity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإخلاص<\/bdi>), and all other obedient deeds entailing forgiveness. Nāfiʿ and Ibn ʿĀmir read the command without the conjunction <strong>wāw<\/strong> (و), treating it as a fresh sentence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استئناف<\/bdi>). And hasten toward “a Garden whose breadth is the heavens and the earth”—if the one were joined to the other. Mention of breadth, rather than length, intensifies its vastness, because breadth is less than length. Another explanation says this does not mean breadth in contrast with length, but simply extraordinary magnitude: it is as great as the seven heavens and seven earths could seem to you; its extent is beyond description. “Prepared” means made ready “for the mindful.” This, Ibn ʿAjība adds, proves that Paradise is already created and lies outside the present cosmos. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N109\">[XIV-N109]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“Hasten” ( وسارعوا ), that is, make haste “toward forgiveness from your Lord,” such as Islam, repentance, sincerity ( الإخلاص ), and all other obedient deeds entailing forgiveness. Nāfiʿ and Ibn ʿĀmir read the command without the conjunction wāw (و), treating it as a fresh sentence ( استئناف ). And hasten toward “a Garden whose breadth is the heavens and the earth”—if the one were joined to the other. Mention of breadth, rather than length, intensifies its vastness, because breadth is less than length. Another explanation says this does not mean breadth in contrast with length, but simply extraordinary magnitude: it is as great as the seven heavens and seven earths could seem to you; its extent is beyond description. “Prepared” means made ready “for the mindful.” This, Ibn ʿAjība adds, proves that Paradise is already created and lies outside the present cosmos. [XIV-N109]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Giving in ease and adversity","html":"<p>Then the Real describes the perfected attributes of those mindful people who will inhabit the Garden: “Those who spend in ease and hardship,” namely in prosperity and adversity and indeed in all circumstances, as is the way of the generous. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is cited as saying: “The Garden is the dwelling of the generous.” Also: “The generous person is close to God, close to the Garden, close to people and far from the Fire; the miser is far from God, far from the Garden, far from people and close to the Fire. An ignorant but generous person is dearer to God than a learned miser.” And again: “Generosity is a tree in Paradise whose branches are in the world. Whoever clings to one of its branches is led by it into Paradise. Miserliness is a tree in the Fire whose branches are in the world; whoever clings to one of those branches is led by it into the Fire.” <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> These are reports quoted by the author, with their individual degrees of authenticity still to be established.<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N110\">[XIV-N110]<\/a><\/p><p>“And those who restrain their anger” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">والكاظمين الغيظ<\/bdi>): they desist from carrying it into effect despite being capable of doing so. He cites the Prophet as saying: “Whoever suppresses anger, while having the power to act upon it, God fills his heart with security and faith.” One poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">When you become angry, be dignified, restraining wrath;<br/>Then you will perceive what you are saying and hearing.<br/>It is honour enough to persevere for an hour,<br/>For which God will be pleased with you and raise you high.<\/blockquote><p>“And those who pardon people,” meaning those who have wronged them. The Prophet is quoted as saying of such people: “In my community these are few, apart from those God has protected, though they were many among the communities that passed.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N111\">[XIV-N111]<\/a><\/p><p>Abū Hurayra related that Abū Bakr sat in the Prophet’s company when a man came and insulted Abū Bakr. While Abū Bakr kept silent, the Prophet smiled. But when Abū Bakr responded to a portion of the abuse, the Prophet became angry and stood up. Abū Bakr followed him, saying, “Messenger of God, he insulted me while you smiled; when I answered him in part, you became angry and rose.” He replied: “While you were silent, an angel was with you answering him. When you spoke, Satan came, and I would not sit in a gathering with Satan. Abū Bakr, three things are true: no servant suffers a wrong and pardons it without God thereby strengthening His support for him; no servant opens the door of begging to increase his wealth without God increasing his want; and no servant opens the door of giving or maintaining kinship without God increasing his abundance thereby.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N112\">[XIV-N112]<\/a><\/p><p>“God loves those who do beautiful good (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الْمُحْسِنِينَ<\/bdi>),” who act well in their dealings with God and with God’s servants. The definite article <strong>al-<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أل<\/bdi>) may be generic (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للجنس<\/bdi>), embracing everyone who practices iḥsān, or it may refer to a known class (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للعهد<\/bdi>), meaning those just described. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N113\">[XIV-N113]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Then the Real describes the perfected attributes of those mindful people who will inhabit the Garden: “Those who spend in ease and hardship,” namely in prosperity and adversity and indeed in all circumstances, as is the way of the generous. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is cited as saying: “The Garden is the dwelling of the generous.” Also: “The generous person is close to God, close to the Garden, close to people and far from the Fire; the miser is far from God, far from the Garden, far from people and close to the Fire. An ignorant but generous person is dearer to God than a learned miser.” And again: “Generosity is a tree in Paradise whose branches are in the world. Whoever clings to one of its branches is led by it into Paradise. Miserliness is a tree in the Fire whose branches are in the world; whoever clings to one of those branches is led by it into the Fire.” Translator’s note: These are reports quoted by the author, with their individual degrees of authenticity still to be established. [XIV-N110] “And those who restrain their anger” ( والكاظمين الغيظ ): they desist from carrying it into effect despite being capable of doing so. He cites the Prophet as saying: “Whoever suppresses anger, while having the power to act upon it, God fills his heart with security and faith.” One poet says: When you become angry, be dignified, restraining wrath; Then you will perceive what you are saying and hearing. It is honour enough to persevere for an hour, For which God will be pleased with you and raise you high. “And those who pardon people,” meaning those who have wronged them. The Prophet is quoted as saying of such people: “In my community these are few, apart from those God has protected, though they were many among the communities that passed.” [XIV-N111] Abū Hurayra related that Abū Bakr sat in the Prophet’s company when a man came and insulted Abū Bakr. While Abū Bakr kept silent, the Prophet smiled. But when Abū Bakr responded to a portion of the abuse, the Prophet became angry and stood up. Abū Bakr followed him, saying, “Messenger of God, he insulted me while you smiled; when I answered him in part, you became angry and rose.” He replied: “While you were silent, an angel was with you answering him. When you spoke, Satan came, and I would not sit in a gathering with Satan. Abū Bakr, three things are true: no servant suffers a wrong and pardons it without God thereby strengthening His support for him; no servant opens the door of begging to increase his wealth without God increasing his want; and no servant opens the door of giving or maintaining kinship without God increasing his abundance thereby.” [XIV-N112] “God loves those who do beautiful good ( الْمُحْسِنِينَ ),” who act well in their dealings with God and with God’s servants. The definite article al- ( أل ) may be generic ( للجنس ), embracing everyone who practices iḥsān, or it may refer to a known class ( للعهد ), meaning those just described. [XIV-N113]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The multiplication of sense-life as an inward form of ribā","html":"<p>Whatever increases the strength of sensory existence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مادة الحس<\/bdi>) is, spiritually speaking, <i>ribā<\/i>: it multiplies the sensory realm and reinforces the substance of heedlessness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مادة الغفلة<\/bdi>). The disciple (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريد<\/bdi>) should not augment it or practise methods that cause it to grow, but should flee its three sources: direct engagement with the sensory realm, thought occupied with it, and speech about it with those devoted to it. Three things strengthen the inward order of meaning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مادة المعنى<\/bdi>): companionship with those who live by inward meaning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل المعنى<\/bdi>), reflection upon meanings (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفكرة في المعاني<\/bdi>), and remembrance of God in the heart (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذكر الله بالقلب<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N114\">[XIV-N114]<\/a><\/p><p>Be mindful of God when engaging with the sensible, “that you may prosper” through reaching the purity of spiritual meanings. Fear the fire of separation prepared for whoever rejects the special spiritual station (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>). “Obey God and the Messenger” in what they have encouraged, “that you may receive mercy,” by enlivening your hearts and spirits with the mysteries of meaning. “Hasten” toward whatever veils your defects, until God’s description conceals your description and His attribute covers yours, thereby bringing you to Him through what comes from Him to you, not through what comes from you to Him. Thus you enter the Garden of direct knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جنة المعارف<\/bdi>), whose expanse of witnessing has no limit, prepared for those who guard against what is other than Him (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتقين السوى<\/bdi>). Such people expend their very lives and property under both Majesty and Beauty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجلال والجمال<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>They “restrain anger,” having mastered their selves and states, and “pardon people,” because, in the uncompromising ascetic idiom quoted here, “the Sufi’s property is freely available and his blood is without recompense” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصوفي ماله مباح ودمه هدر<\/bdi>). Some Sufis used to say: “If you wish to know the condition of a spiritual poor person (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال الفقير<\/bdi>), anger him and see what emerges.” The shaykh of our shaykhs said: “The pivot of Sufism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قطب التصوف<\/bdi>) is: do not grow angry and do not cause anger.” End of quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N115\">[XIV-N115]<\/a><\/p><p>These verses are ascribed to ʿUrwa ibn al-Zubayr:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">People, however noble their stock, do not reach honour<br/>Until they abase themselves before others, though they were mighty;<br/>And when they are insulted, their faces remain bright—<br/>Not the pardon of weakness, but the pardon of self-command.<\/blockquote><p>“God loves those who do beautiful good”: those who have attained the station of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مقام الإحسان<\/bdi>) and worship God through witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>). Their good reaches those who wrong them and those who treat them well, humankind and jinn alike. Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī said: “Iḥsān is for one’s beneficence to be general, not like the sun, the wind and the rain”—meaning, as Ibn ʿAjība explains, not to favour one land and exclude another. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> Cairo transmits a negative comparison with the sun, wind and rain, followed by Ibn ʿAjība’s gloss about favouring one land over another. An earlier version attributed to al-Ḥasan in al-Thaʿlabī’s al-Kashf wa-l-bayān (Ibn ʿĀshūr ed., first edition, 1422/2002, III:167; printed page inspected) says that beneficence should be general and not exclusive, like wind, sun and rain. This is a material difference between the two texts. The translation retains Cairo’s wording and explanatory gloss; independent manuscripts are needed to determine whether the change is authorial or arose in transmission.<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N116\">[XIV-N116]<\/a><\/p><p>Sufyān al-Thawrī said: “Iḥsān is not to do good to the one who does good to you; it is to do good to the one who wrongs you. Doing good to a benefactor is commerce, like the ready money of the market: take from me and give me in return.” Al-Sarī al-Saqaṭī said: “Iḥsān is to do good when one can, for one cannot do good at every moment.” They recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The making of deeds of kindness is not possible<br/>At every moment and in every hour;<br/>So when the opportunity presents itself, hasten to it,<br/>Lest that opportunity disappear.<\/blockquote><p>Al-Wartajbī said about “Hasten toward forgiveness from your Lord and a Garden ...”: the Real knows creatures’ ailments and their inclination toward what their lower selves desire, so He summoned them, through obedience to Him, toward these two aims—pardon and Paradise. He summoned the elect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخاصة<\/bdi>) to Himself, saying: “Flee to God” [Q 51:50]. He then showed that all beings lie within the ordeal of culpability. Through the verse, he attributed a fault to everyone: though some are protected from slipping, their deficiency is their inadequate recognition of the full majesty due to God. As the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Were God to punish the angels, He would be justified.” It was asked, “But they are protected from sin.” He replied, blessings and peace be upon him: “Because of how little they know their Lord.” Accordingly He called them to forgiveness. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> The quotation and its attribution to the Prophet reproduce the author’s cited exposition. The dialogue also appears in Rūzbihān’s <i>ʿArāʾis al-bayān<\/i> (al-Mazīdī ed., 1429/2008, I:196; printed page inspected). Its modern footnote cites Ibn ʿAjība’s <i>Baḥr<\/i> (1/336), so that note supplies no independent authentication. The Cairo editor likewise reports that he did not locate the report.<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N117\">[XIV-N117]<\/a><\/p><p><span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: The Arabic repeats <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عِلَل<\/bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلتين<\/bdi> (ʿilal, “ailments,” and al-ʿillatayn, “the two motives/causes”). Rūzbihān’s fuller source passage contrasts these desired benefits with seeking God Himself and then calls seeking forgiveness a pursuit of the self’s portion (<i>ʿArāʾis al-bayān<\/i>, al-Mazīdī ed., first edition, 1429/2008, I:196, on 3:133; printed page inspected). Thus “aims” here carries a suggestion of motives that still concern the self. The prepositions distinguish the means—obedience—from the two ends sought.]<\/span><\/p><p>The marginal commentator explains: saying that the verse attributes a fault to all creatures refers to the inclusion, in “He forgives whom He wills,” of those in the heavens, which includes the angels. Forgiveness presupposes fault, but each creature has a deficiency appropriate to it. The summons to forgiveness is apparently taken from “Hasten toward forgiveness from your Lord,” with its address understood to include those in heaven. Even the angels may be thought of as judging by outward appearances, disagreeing and disputing, matters that allow for error and occasion forgiveness. Moreover, falling short of knowing the full measure of God’s Majesty is a shortcoming no creature can escape, for comprehending Him through knowledge is impossible. For this reason there can never, for all eternity, be a final limit to advancement in knowledge of Him (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي في المعرفة<\/bdi>). End of marginal gloss. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N118\">[XIV-N118]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whatever increases the strength of sensory existence ( مادة الحس ) is, spiritually speaking, ribā : it multiplies the sensory realm and reinforces the substance of heedlessness ( مادة الغفلة ). The disciple ( المريد ) should not augment it or practise methods that cause it to grow, but should flee its three sources: direct engagement with the sensory realm, thought occupied with it, and speech about it with those devoted to it. Three things strengthen the inward order of meaning ( مادة المعنى ): companionship with those who live by inward meaning ( أهل المعنى ), reflection upon meanings ( الفكرة في المعاني ), and remembrance of God in the heart ( ذكر الله بالقلب ). [XIV-N114] Be mindful of God when engaging with the sensible, “that you may prosper” through reaching the purity of spiritual meanings. Fear the fire of separation prepared for whoever rejects the special spiritual station ( الخصوصية ). “Obey God and the Messenger” in what they have encouraged, “that you may receive mercy,” by enlivening your hearts and spirits with the mysteries of meaning. “Hasten” toward whatever veils your defects, until God’s description conceals your description and His attribute covers yours, thereby bringing you to Him through what comes from Him to you, not through what comes from you to Him. Thus you enter the Garden of direct knowledge ( جنة المعارف ), whose expanse of witnessing has no limit, prepared for those who guard against what is other than Him ( المتقين السوى ). Such people expend their very lives and property under both Majesty and Beauty ( الجلال والجمال ). They “restrain anger,” having mastered their selves and states, and “pardon people,” because, in the uncompromising ascetic idiom quoted here, “the Sufi’s property is freely available and his blood is without recompense” ( الصوفي ماله مباح ودمه هدر ). Some Sufis used to say: “If you wish to know the condition of a spiritual poor person ( حال الفقير ), anger him and see what emerges.” The shaykh of our shaykhs said: “The pivot of Sufism ( قطب التصوف ) is: do not grow angry and do not cause anger.” End of quotation. [XIV-N115] These verses are ascribed to ʿUrwa ibn al-Zubayr: People, however noble their stock, do not reach honour Until they abase themselves before others, though they were mighty; And when they are insulted, their faces remain bright— Not the pardon of weakness, but the pardon of self-command. “God loves those who do beautiful good”: those who have attained the station of spiritual excellence ( مقام الإحسان ) and worship God through witnessing and direct vision ( الشهود والعيان ). Their good reaches those who wrong them and those who treat them well, humankind and jinn alike. Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī said: “Iḥsān is for one’s beneficence to be general, not like the sun, the wind and the rain”—meaning, as Ibn ʿAjība explains, not to favour one land and exclude another. Translator’s note: Cairo transmits a negative comparison with the sun, wind and rain, followed by Ibn ʿAjība’s gloss about favouring one land over another. An earlier version attributed to al-Ḥasan in al-Thaʿlabī’s al-Kashf wa-l-bayān (Ibn ʿĀshūr ed., first edition, 1422/2002, III:167; printed page inspected) says that beneficence should be general and not exclusive, like wind, sun and rain. This is a material difference between the two texts. The translation retains Cairo’s wording and explanatory gloss; independent manuscripts are needed to determine whether the change is authorial or arose in transmission. [XIV-N116] Sufyān al-Thawrī said: “Iḥsān is not to do good to the one who does good to you; it is to do good to the one who wrongs you. Doing good to a benefactor is commerce, like the ready money of the market: take from me and give me in return.” Al-Sarī al-Saqaṭī said: “Iḥsān is to do good when one can, for one cannot do good at every moment.” They recited: The making of deeds of kindness is not possible At every moment and in every hour; So when the opportunity presents itself, hasten to it, Lest that opportunity disappear. Al-Wartajbī said about “Hasten toward forgiveness from your Lord and a Garden ...”: the Real knows creatures’ ailments and their inclination toward what their lower selves desire, so He summoned them, through obedience to Him, toward these two aims—pardon and Paradise. He summoned the elect ( الخاصة ) to Himself, saying: “Flee to God” [Q 51:50]. He then showed that all beings lie within the ordeal of culpability. Through the verse, he attributed a fault to everyone: though some are protected from slipping, their deficiency is their inadequate recognition of the full majesty due to God. As the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, said: “Were God to punish the angels, He would be justified.” It was asked, “But they are protected from sin.” He replied, blessings and peace be upon him: “Because of how little they know their Lord.” Accordingly He called them to forgiveness. Translator’s note: The quotation and its attribution to the Prophet reproduce the author’s cited exposition. The dialogue also appears in Rūzbihān’s ʿArāʾis al-bayān (al-Mazīdī ed., 1429/2008, I:196; printed page inspected). Its modern footnote cites Ibn ʿAjība’s Baḥr (1/336), so that note supplies no independent authentication. The Cairo editor likewise reports that he did not locate the report. [XIV-N117] [Translator’s note: The Arabic repeats عِلَل and العلتين (ʿilal, “ailments,” and al-ʿillatayn, “the two motives/causes”). Rūzbihān’s fuller source passage contrasts these desired benefits with seeking God Himself and then calls seeking forgiveness a pursuit of the self’s portion ( ʿArāʾis al-bayān , al-Mazīdī ed., first edition, 1429/2008, I:196, on 3:133; printed page inspected). Thus “aims” here carries a suggestion of motives that still concern the self. The prepositions distinguish the means—obedience—from the two ends sought.] The marginal commentator explains: saying that the verse attributes a fault to all creatures refers to the inclusion, in “He forgives whom He wills,” of those in the heavens, which includes the angels. Forgiveness presupposes fault, but each creature has a deficiency appropriate to it. The summons to forgiveness is apparently taken from “Hasten toward forgiveness from your Lord,” with its address understood to include those in heaven. Even the angels may be thought of as judging by outward appearances, disagreeing and disputing, matters that allow for error and occasion forgiveness. Moreover, falling short of knowing the full measure of God’s Majesty is a shortcoming no creature can escape, for comprehending Him through knowledge is impossible. For this reason there can never, for all eternity, be a final limit to advancement in knowledge of Him ( الترقي في المعرفة ). End of marginal gloss. [XIV-N118]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He next describes the condition of the People of the Right (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل اليمين<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He next describes the condition of the People of the Right ( أهل اليمين )."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C407.1","page":407,"ar":"البيتان لأبي القاسم بن حبيب، كما في تفسير البحر المحيط: 3/63.","en":"The two verses are by Abū al-Qāsim ibn Ḥabīb, as stated in the commentary al-Baḥr al-muḥīṭ, III:63.","anchor":"يَرْضَى بِهَا عَنْكَ الإلَهُ ويَرْفَعُ"},{"id":"C408.1","page":408,"ar":"الأبيات لأبي العباس الحماني، كما ذكر القرطبي في تفسيره.","en":"The verses are by Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Ḥimmānī, as al-Qurṭubī states in his commentary.","anchor":"حذَراً من تعذّر الإمكان"},{"id":"C409.1","page":409,"ar":"لم أقف عليه. وذكر المتقي الهندي في الكنز حديث: «لو أن الله عذب أهل سماواته وأهل أرضه لعذبهم وهو غير ظالم لهم، ولو رحمهم لكانت رحمته لهم خيرا لهم من أعمالهم..»، وعزاه لأحمد وعبد بن حميد والترمذي وابن حبان. انظر: (الكنز 1/130 ح 613).","en":"I have not located it. Al-Muttaqī al-Hindī mentions in al-Kanz the report: “Were God to punish the inhabitants of His heavens and earth, He would punish them without wronging them; and were He to show them mercy, His mercy would be better for them than their deeds….” He attributes it to Aḥmad, ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd, al-Tirmidhī and Ibn Ḥibbān. See al-Kanz, I:130, no. 613.","anchor":"من قلة معرفتهم بربهم"}],"english_characters":12645,"arabic_characters":6977},{"id":"q3-135-136","q":"3:135–136","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:135–136 · Repentance, persistence in sin and eight named fruits of spiritual witnessing","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 409–411","cairo":"I:409–411","source_page_first":409,"source_page_last":411,"ar":"وَالَّذِينَ إِذا فَعَلُوا فاحِشَةً أَوْ ظَلَمُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ ذَكَرُوا اللَّهَ فَاسْتَغْفَرُوا لِذُنُوبِهِمْ وَمَنْ يَغْفِرُ الذُّنُوبَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَلَمْ يُصِرُّوا عَلى ما فَعَلُوا وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (135) أُولئِكَ جَزاؤُهُمْ مَغْفِرَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ وَجَنَّاتٌ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها وَنِعْمَ أَجْرُ الْعامِلِينَ (136)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَالَّذِينَ إِذا فَعَلُوا فاحِشَةً أي: فعلة بالغة في الفحش والقبح، كالزنى، أَوْ ظَلَمُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ بأي ذنب كان، أو فعلوا كبيرة أو صغيرة، أو الفاحشة: ما يتعدى للغير، وظلم النفس ما يخص، أو الفاحشة بالفعل، وظلم النفس بالقول، ذَكَرُوا اللَّهَ أي: عقابه وغضبه وعرضه الأكبر، أو ذَكَرُوا اللَّهَ في أنفسهم أن الله سائلهم عنه، أو كونه رقيباً عليهم، أو ذَكَرُوا اللَّهَ باللسان فَاسْتَغْفَرُوا لِذُنُوبِهِمْ بالندم والتوبة، وَمَنْ يَغْفِرُ الذُّنُوبَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ أي: لا أحد يغفره إلا الله، والمراد: وصفه تعالى بسعة الرحمة وعموم المغفرة، والحث على الاستغفار.\nوَلَمْ يُصِرُّوا عَلى ما فَعَلُوا أي: لم يدوموا عليها غير مستغفرين، لقوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «ما أصَرَّ مَن اسْتَغفَر، ولو عَادَ في اليَوم سَبْعِينَ مَرَةً» ، وذلك إذا صحبه الندم، وقال أيضاً: «لا كَبِيرةَ مَعَ الاستغفَار، ولا صغيرة مع\nالإصْرارِ» . قال قتادة: إياكم والإصرار، فإنما هلك المصرون الماضون قِدْماً في معاصي الله تعالى، لم يتوبوا حتى أتاهم الموت. هـ. وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ أن الإصرار يضر بهم، أو: وهم يعلمون أن لهم رباً يغفر الذنب لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «مَنْ أَذْنَبَ ذنْباً، وعَلِمَ أَنَّ له ربّاً يَغْفُرِ الذنوب، غَفَرَ له وإِنْ لمْ يسْتَغفر» . وفي الحديث القدسي يقول الله تعالى: «مِنْ علم أني ذو قدرة على المغفرة غفرتُ له ولا أبالي» . وفي بعض الكتب المنزلة:\n«يا ابْنَ آدَمَ، إِنَّكَ ما دَعَوْتني ورَجَوْتني لأغفرن لَكَ على مَا كَانَ منكَ ولا أُبَالِي» . أو: (وهم يعلمون) أن التوبة تمحق الذنوب.\nأُولئِكَ جَزاؤُهُمْ مَغْفِرَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ تغطية لذنوبهم، وَجَنَّاتٌ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها، ولا يلزم من إعدادها للتائبين اختصاصهم بها، كما لا يلزم من إعداد النار للكفار اختصاصهم بها، ثم مدح أجر التائبين فقال: وَنِعْمَ أَجْرُ الْعامِلِينَ، وانظر هذا الفرق العظيم الذي بين المحسنين وأهل اليمين، قال في الآية الأولى: وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ وقال في هذه الآية: وَنِعْمَ أَجْرُ الْعامِلِينَ، أهل الآية الأولى من خواص الأحباب، وأهل هذه يأخذون أجرهم من وراء الباب. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى عين التحقيق.\nالإشارة: أهل مقام الإحسان عملهم قلبي، كالسخاء والعفو وكظم الغيظ، وأهل اليمين عملهم بدني، بين طاعة ومعصية وغفلة ويقظة، إذا فعلوا فاحشة تابوا واستغفروا، وإذا فعلوا طاعة فرحوا واستبشروا، أهل مقام الإحسان غائبون عن رؤية أعمالهم ووجودهم، وأهل اليمين معتمدون على أعمالهم، إذا فعلوا طاعة قوى رجاؤهم، وإذا زلَّوا نقص رجاؤهم، أهل مقام الإحسان فانون عن أنفسهم باقون بربهم، وأهل اليمين أنفسهم موجودة وأعمالهم لديهم مشهودة، أهل مقام الإحسان محبوبون، وأهل اليمين مُحِبُّون، أهل مقام الإحسان فنيت عندهم الرسوم والأشكال، وبقي في نظرهم وجود الكبير المتعال، وأهل اليمين: الأكوان عندهم موجودة، وشموس المعارف عن قلوبهم مفقودة، أهل مقام الإحسان يعبدون الله على نعت الشهود والعيان، وأهل اليمين يعبدون الله من وراء حجاب الدليل والبرهان، أهل الدليل والبرهان عموم عند أهل الشهود والعيان.\nواعلم أن لمعرفة الشهود والعيان ثمرات ونتائج، حصرها بعضهم في إحدى عشرة خصلة:\nالأولى: الحرية، ومعناها أن يكون العارف فرداً لِفَرْدٍ، من غير أن يكون تحت رق شيء من الموجودات، لا من أغراض الدنيا ولا من أغراض الآخرة، فالحرية عبارة عن غاية التصفية والطهارة. قال بعضهم: ليس بحُرٍّ من بقي عليه من تصفية نفسه مقدار فص نواة، المكاتب عبد ما بقي عليه درهم.\nالثانية: الوجود، وهو الفوز بحقيقة الأشياء في الأصل، وهو عبارة عن إدراك مقام تضمحل فيه الرسوم، بالاستغراق في الحقيقة الأزلية.\nالثالثة: الجمع الأتم، وهو الحال الذي يقضي بقطع الإشارات، والشخوص عن الأمارات والعلامات، بعد صحة التمكين والبراءة من التلوين.\nالرابعة: الصحو، وهو عبارة عن تمكين حال المشاهدة، واتصالها، مع برء الروح من لدغات الدَّهَشِ، ولا يكمل الصحو إلا بحياة الروح بوارد الجمع الدائم.\nالخامسة: التحقيق، وهو الوصول إلى المعرفة بالله، التي لا تدْركُ بالحواس، لتخليص المشرب من الحق بالحق في الحق، حتى تسقط المشاهدات، وتبطل العبارات، وتفنى الإشارات.\nالسادسة: البسط، ونعني به: بسط الروح باسترسال شهود المعاني عند سقوط الأواني، وفي ذلك يقول ابن الفارض:\nفما سكَنتْ والهمَ يوماً بموضِعٍ ... كذلك لم يسكُنْ مع النغَم الغَمُّ\nالسابعة: التلبيس، وهو تغطية الأسرار بأستار الأسباب، إبقاء للحكمة وستراً عن العامة.\nالثامنة: البقاء، والمراد به الخروج عن فناء المشاهدة إلى بقاء المعرفة، من غير أفول يخل بشمس المشاهدة، ولا رجوع إلى شواهد الحس، إنما هو استصحاب الجمع مع استئناس الروح بحلاوة المعاني، فهو كبائِن دانٍ. انظر بقيتها فى [بغية السالك] . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم قوّى قلوب أهل أحد لمّا انكسرت بالهزيمة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَالَّذِينَ إِذا فَعَلُوا فاحِشَةً أَوْ ظَلَمُوا أَنْفُسَهُمْ ذَكَرُوا اللَّهَ فَاسْتَغْفَرُوا لِذُنُوبِهِمْ وَمَنْ يَغْفِرُ الذُّنُوبَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَلَمْ يُصِرُّوا عَلى ما فَعَلُوا وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (135) أُولئِكَ جَزاؤُهُمْ مَغْفِرَةٌ مِنْ رَبِّهِمْ وَجَنَّاتٌ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها وَنِعْمَ أَجْرُ الْعامِلِينَ (136)","quran_en":"135. And those who, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves, remember God and seek forgiveness for their sins—and who forgives sins but God?—and do not knowingly persist in what they have done: 136. Their recompense is forgiveness from their Lord and Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein. How excellent is the reward of those who work!","segments":[{"type":"theology","title":"Indecency, wrongdoing and the refusal to persist in sin","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Those who, when they commit an indecency (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاحشة<\/bdi>),” that is, an act of extreme obscenity or ugliness, such as illicit sexual intercourse (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الزنا<\/bdi>), “or wrong themselves,” through any sin whatsoever. This may distinguish major and minor sins; or an indecency affecting another person as against wrongdoing confined to oneself; or indecency enacted through deeds as against wrongdoing committed in speech.<\/p><p>“They remember God”: His punishment, wrath and the Great Presentation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العرض الأكبر<\/bdi>); or they call to mind that God will interrogate them, and that He observes them; or they remember God with the tongue. “Then they seek forgiveness for their sins” through remorse and repentance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الندم والتوبة<\/bdi>). “And who forgives sins but God?” No one other than God forgives them. Its purpose is to describe the breadth of His mercy, the universality of His forgiveness and the encouragement to seek it.<\/p><p>“And they do not persist in what they have done,” that is, they do not continue in it without asking forgiveness. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is quoted as saying: “One who seeks forgiveness has not persisted [in sin], even if he returns to it seventy times in a day,” provided that his seeking forgiveness is joined with remorse. Another reported saying runs: “There is no major sin in the presence of seeking forgiveness, and no minor sin in the presence of persistence.” Qatāda warned: “Beware of persistence: those who persisted before you were ruined by advancing further in disobedience to God, without repenting until death came to them.” End of quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N119\">[XIV-N119]<\/a><\/p><p>“While they know” that persistence injures them; or while they know they have a Lord who forgives sin. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Whoever commits a sin and knows that he has a Lord who forgives sins is forgiven, even if he has not expressly asked forgiveness.” A divine saying (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حديث قدسي<\/bdi>) is also cited: “Whoever knows that I possess the power to forgive, I forgive him and do not mind.” In some revealed books there is the declaration: “Son of Adam, so long as you call upon Me and place your hope in Me, I will surely forgive you for whatever you have done, and I do not mind.” Alternatively, “while they know” refers to their recognition that repentance erases sins. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> The reports are transcribed here as cited; the wording, attribution and transmission of each require independent hadith review.<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N120\">[XIV-N120]<\/a><\/p><p>“Their recompense is forgiveness from their Lord,” the covering over of their sins, “and Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein.” The fact that Paradise is prepared for the repentant does not mean that it is exclusively theirs, just as the preparation of the Fire for unbelievers does not mean it is exclusively theirs. He then praises the recompense of the repentant: “How excellent is the reward of those who work!”<\/p><p>Contemplate the enormous distinction between those who practice iḥsān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحسنون<\/bdi>) and the People of the Right (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل اليمين<\/bdi>). Of the former He says, “God loves the doers of beautiful good”; of the latter, “How excellent is the reward of those who work.” The people of the first verse belong among the beloved elect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خواص الأحباب<\/bdi>), whereas those of the present verse receive their reward from beyond the door. God grants success, and He guides to the very heart of verification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين التحقيق<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N121\">[XIV-N121]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Those who, when they commit an indecency ( فاحشة ),” that is, an act of extreme obscenity or ugliness, such as illicit sexual intercourse ( الزنا ), “or wrong themselves,” through any sin whatsoever. This may distinguish major and minor sins; or an indecency affecting another person as against wrongdoing confined to oneself; or indecency enacted through deeds as against wrongdoing committed in speech. “They remember God”: His punishment, wrath and the Great Presentation ( العرض الأكبر ); or they call to mind that God will interrogate them, and that He observes them; or they remember God with the tongue. “Then they seek forgiveness for their sins” through remorse and repentance ( الندم والتوبة ). “And who forgives sins but God?” No one other than God forgives them. Its purpose is to describe the breadth of His mercy, the universality of His forgiveness and the encouragement to seek it. “And they do not persist in what they have done,” that is, they do not continue in it without asking forgiveness. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is quoted as saying: “One who seeks forgiveness has not persisted [in sin], even if he returns to it seventy times in a day,” provided that his seeking forgiveness is joined with remorse. Another reported saying runs: “There is no major sin in the presence of seeking forgiveness, and no minor sin in the presence of persistence.” Qatāda warned: “Beware of persistence: those who persisted before you were ruined by advancing further in disobedience to God, without repenting until death came to them.” End of quotation. [XIV-N119] “While they know” that persistence injures them; or while they know they have a Lord who forgives sin. The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Whoever commits a sin and knows that he has a Lord who forgives sins is forgiven, even if he has not expressly asked forgiveness.” A divine saying ( حديث قدسي ) is also cited: “Whoever knows that I possess the power to forgive, I forgive him and do not mind.” In some revealed books there is the declaration: “Son of Adam, so long as you call upon Me and place your hope in Me, I will surely forgive you for whatever you have done, and I do not mind.” Alternatively, “while they know” refers to their recognition that repentance erases sins. Translator’s note: The reports are transcribed here as cited; the wording, attribution and transmission of each require independent hadith review. [XIV-N120] “Their recompense is forgiveness from their Lord,” the covering over of their sins, “and Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein.” The fact that Paradise is prepared for the repentant does not mean that it is exclusively theirs, just as the preparation of the Fire for unbelievers does not mean it is exclusively theirs. He then praises the recompense of the repentant: “How excellent is the reward of those who work!” Contemplate the enormous distinction between those who practice iḥsān ( المحسنون ) and the People of the Right ( أهل اليمين ). Of the former He says, “God loves the doers of beautiful good”; of the latter, “How excellent is the reward of those who work.” The people of the first verse belong among the beloved elect ( خواص الأحباب ), whereas those of the present verse receive their reward from beyond the door. God grants success, and He guides to the very heart of verification ( عين التحقيق ). [XIV-N121]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"People of spiritual excellence and People of the Right","html":"<p>For the people of the station of iḥsān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل مقام الإحسان<\/bdi>), work belongs principally to the heart: generosity, forgiveness and restraining anger. For the People of the Right, work belongs to the bodily life, alternating between obedience and disobedience, heedlessness and wakefulness. When they commit indecency they repent and seek forgiveness; when they obey they rejoice and take comfort.<\/p><p>The people of iḥsān are absent from contemplating their works and their separate existence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غائبون عن رؤية أعمالهم ووجودهم<\/bdi>). The People of the Right rely upon their works: when they obey, hope grows; when they slip, hope diminishes. The people of iḥsān are effaced from themselves (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فانون عن أنفسهم<\/bdi>), subsisting through their Lord (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">باقون بربهم<\/bdi>); the People of the Right remain conscious of their individual selves and observe their own works. The people of iḥsān are beloved (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محبوبون<\/bdi>), while the People of the Right are lovers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مُحِبُّون<\/bdi>). For the first, formal marks and shapes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرسوم والأشكال<\/bdi>) vanish, and they behold only the existence of the Great and Exalted One (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وجود الكبير المتعال<\/bdi>). For the second, the created world still stands as independently present before them and the suns of gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شموس المعارف<\/bdi>) are absent from their hearts. The people of iḥsān worship through witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>); the People of the Right worship from behind the veil of demonstration and discursive argument (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الدليل والبرهان<\/bdi>). From the viewpoint of those who witness directly, those who have only proof and argument are counted among the generality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العموم<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N122\">[XIV-N122]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"For the people of the station of iḥsān ( أهل مقام الإحسان ), work belongs principally to the heart: generosity, forgiveness and restraining anger. For the People of the Right, work belongs to the bodily life, alternating between obedience and disobedience, heedlessness and wakefulness. When they commit indecency they repent and seek forgiveness; when they obey they rejoice and take comfort. The people of iḥsān are absent from contemplating their works and their separate existence ( غائبون عن رؤية أعمالهم ووجودهم ). The People of the Right rely upon their works: when they obey, hope grows; when they slip, hope diminishes. The people of iḥsān are effaced from themselves ( فانون عن أنفسهم ), subsisting through their Lord ( باقون بربهم ); the People of the Right remain conscious of their individual selves and observe their own works. The people of iḥsān are beloved ( محبوبون ), while the People of the Right are lovers ( مُحِبُّون ). For the first, formal marks and shapes ( الرسوم والأشكال ) vanish, and they behold only the existence of the Great and Exalted One ( وجود الكبير المتعال ). For the second, the created world still stands as independently present before them and the suns of gnosis ( شموس المعارف ) are absent from their hearts. The people of iḥsān worship through witnessing and direct vision ( الشهود والعيان ); the People of the Right worship from behind the veil of demonstration and discursive argument ( الدليل والبرهان ). From the viewpoint of those who witness directly, those who have only proof and argument are counted among the generality ( العموم ). [XIV-N122]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Fruits of knowledge through witnessing: eight of eleven are given here","html":"<p>Know that the knowledge of witnessing and direct vision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة الشهود والعيان<\/bdi>) bears fruits and consequences (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ثمرات ونتائج<\/bdi>). One author has enumerated <strong>eleven qualities<\/strong>; Ibn ʿAjība states eight here and directs the reader to <i>Bughyat al-Sālik<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بغية السالك<\/bdi>) for the rest. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N123\">[XIV-N123]<\/a><\/p><p><strong>First: Freedom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحرية<\/bdi>).<\/strong> The knower (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العارف<\/bdi>) becomes a solitary servant of the One Alone (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فرداً لِفَرْد<\/bdi>), without falling under the bondage of any existent thing, neither worldly aims nor aims attached to the Hereafter. Freedom thus means the ultimate refinement and purification of the self (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غاية التصفية والطهارة<\/bdi>). Someone said: “One who still has as much left to purify in his soul as can be sucked from a date-stone is not free.” A slave buying his freedom through a manumission contract (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكاتب<\/bdi>) remains a slave as long as a single dirham is outstanding.<\/p><p><span class=\"translator-note\">[Translator’s note: Cairo I:410 prints <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فص نواة<\/bdi> (faṣṣ nawāt). The translation follows the source reading <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مص نواة<\/bdi> (maṣṣ nawāt, “sucking a date-stone”), physically verified in al-Sāḥilī’s <i>Bughyat al-sālik<\/i> (al-Mazīdī ed., Beirut, 1440/2019, p. 258). The earlier <i>Risāla<\/i> of al-Qushayrī, chapter on freedom (II:372, Dār al-Maʿārif; electronic primary text), likewise has <i>maṣṣ<\/i> in a question put to al-Junayd and follows it with the manumission comparison. An electronic <i>Bughya<\/i> apparatus reports omission of <i>maṣṣ<\/i> in witness B; that apparatus is not reproduced in the 2019 edition, and no image of B has been checked. The English expands the image of the slightest remaining worldly attachment. This remains a disclosed source-based correction of Cairo, not manuscript proof of Ibn ʿAjība’s wording; <i>faṣṣ<\/i> is preserved in the Arabic transcription.]<\/span><\/p><p><strong>Second: Finding or realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوجود<\/bdi>).<\/strong> Attainment of the reality of things in their origin (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفوز بحقيقة الأشياء في الأصل<\/bdi>), namely realizing a station in which formal traces (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرسوم<\/bdi>) are effaced through total immersion in the pre-eternal Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة الأزلية<\/bdi>).<\/p><p><strong>Third: Complete gathering (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجمع الأتم<\/bdi>).<\/strong> A state that entails ceasing to indicate by signs and passing beyond visible indicators and marks (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأمارات والعلامات<\/bdi>), after stable realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التمكين<\/bdi>) and freedom from variability of spiritual state (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التلوين<\/bdi>).<\/p><p><strong>Fourth: Sobriety (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصحو<\/bdi>).<\/strong> The stabilized and enduring state of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تمكين حال المشاهدة واتصالها<\/bdi>), in which the spirit is healed of the piercing shocks of bewilderment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الدَّهَش<\/bdi>). Sobriety does not reach perfection unless the spirit is enlivened through the continual influx of gathering (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وارد الجمع الدائم<\/bdi>).<\/p><p><strong>Fifth: Verification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التحقيق<\/bdi>).<\/strong> Arrival at knowledge of God that cannot be grasped through the senses: the spiritual draught is purified “from the Real, through the Real, within the Real” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">من الحق بالحق في الحق<\/bdi>), until acts of witnessing fall away, verbal expressions lose their hold, and indicative gestures are effaced.<\/p><p><strong>Sixth: Expansion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البسط<\/bdi>).<\/strong> The freeing of the spirit through an unbroken witnessing of meanings when the containers of sense (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأواني<\/bdi>) have fallen away. Ibn al-Fāriḍ says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Never has [this wine] dwelt with sorrow in one place for a day;<br/>Thus distress cannot remain where melodies sound.<\/blockquote><p><strong>Seventh: Veiling or disguise (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التلبيس<\/bdi>).<\/strong> The covering of spiritual mysteries (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأسرار<\/bdi>) beneath the outward veils of causes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أستار الأسباب<\/bdi>), so that the wisdom of the ordering of things may remain in place and the inner matters be hidden from the common people.<\/p><p><strong>Eighth: Subsistence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البقاء<\/bdi>).<\/strong> Emerging from the effacement involved in witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فناء المشاهدة<\/bdi>) into the abiding permanence of knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بقاء المعرفة<\/bdi>), without any setting that dims the sun of witnessing and without returning to the evidences of sense. Rather, it is a carrying-forward of gathering (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استصحاب الجمع<\/bdi>), while the spirit takes delight in the sweetness of meanings. Thus the realized person is “separate yet near” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كبائن دانٍ<\/bdi>). Consult <i>Bughyat al-Sālik<\/i> for the remaining qualities. And God grants success. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N123\">[XIV-N123]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Know that the knowledge of witnessing and direct vision ( معرفة الشهود والعيان ) bears fruits and consequences ( ثمرات ونتائج ). One author has enumerated eleven qualities ; Ibn ʿAjība states eight here and directs the reader to Bughyat al-Sālik ( بغية السالك ) for the rest. [XIV-N123] First: Freedom ( الحرية ). The knower ( العارف ) becomes a solitary servant of the One Alone ( فرداً لِفَرْد ), without falling under the bondage of any existent thing, neither worldly aims nor aims attached to the Hereafter. Freedom thus means the ultimate refinement and purification of the self ( غاية التصفية والطهارة ). Someone said: “One who still has as much left to purify in his soul as can be sucked from a date-stone is not free.” A slave buying his freedom through a manumission contract ( المكاتب ) remains a slave as long as a single dirham is outstanding. [Translator’s note: Cairo I:410 prints فص نواة (faṣṣ nawāt). The translation follows the source reading مص نواة (maṣṣ nawāt, “sucking a date-stone”), physically verified in al-Sāḥilī’s Bughyat al-sālik (al-Mazīdī ed., Beirut, 1440/2019, p. 258). The earlier Risāla of al-Qushayrī, chapter on freedom (II:372, Dār al-Maʿārif; electronic primary text), likewise has maṣṣ in a question put to al-Junayd and follows it with the manumission comparison. An electronic Bughya apparatus reports omission of maṣṣ in witness B; that apparatus is not reproduced in the 2019 edition, and no image of B has been checked. The English expands the image of the slightest remaining worldly attachment. This remains a disclosed source-based correction of Cairo, not manuscript proof of Ibn ʿAjība’s wording; faṣṣ is preserved in the Arabic transcription.] Second: Finding or realization ( الوجود ). Attainment of the reality of things in their origin ( الفوز بحقيقة الأشياء في الأصل ), namely realizing a station in which formal traces ( الرسوم ) are effaced through total immersion in the pre-eternal Reality ( الحقيقة الأزلية ). Third: Complete gathering ( الجمع الأتم ). A state that entails ceasing to indicate by signs and passing beyond visible indicators and marks ( الأمارات والعلامات ), after stable realization ( التمكين ) and freedom from variability of spiritual state ( التلوين ). Fourth: Sobriety ( الصحو ). The stabilized and enduring state of witnessing ( تمكين حال المشاهدة واتصالها ), in which the spirit is healed of the piercing shocks of bewilderment ( الدَّهَش ). Sobriety does not reach perfection unless the spirit is enlivened through the continual influx of gathering ( وارد الجمع الدائم ). Fifth: Verification ( التحقيق ). Arrival at knowledge of God that cannot be grasped through the senses: the spiritual draught is purified “from the Real, through the Real, within the Real” ( من الحق بالحق في الحق ), until acts of witnessing fall away, verbal expressions lose their hold, and indicative gestures are effaced. Sixth: Expansion ( البسط ). The freeing of the spirit through an unbroken witnessing of meanings when the containers of sense ( الأواني ) have fallen away. Ibn al-Fāriḍ says: Never has [this wine] dwelt with sorrow in one place for a day; Thus distress cannot remain where melodies sound. Seventh: Veiling or disguise ( التلبيس ). The covering of spiritual mysteries ( الأسرار ) beneath the outward veils of causes ( أستار الأسباب ), so that the wisdom of the ordering of things may remain in place and the inner matters be hidden from the common people. Eighth: Subsistence ( البقاء ). Emerging from the effacement involved in witnessing ( فناء المشاهدة ) into the abiding permanence of knowledge ( بقاء المعرفة ), without any setting that dims the sun of witnessing and without returning to the evidences of sense. Rather, it is a carrying-forward of gathering ( استصحاب الجمع ), while the spirit takes delight in the sweetness of meanings. Thus the realized person is “separate yet near” ( كبائن دانٍ ). Consult Bughyat al-Sālik for the remaining qualities. And God grants success. [XIV-N123]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then strengthened the hearts of the people of Uḥud after they had been broken by defeat.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then strengthened the hearts of the people of Uḥud after they had been broken by defeat."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":9070,"arabic_characters":4494},{"id":"q3-137-139","q":"3:137–139","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:137–139 · Lessons from earlier nations and strength after defeat","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 411–412","cairo":"I:411–412","source_page_first":411,"source_page_last":412,"ar":"قَدْ خَلَتْ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ سُنَنٌ فَسِيرُوا فِي الْأَرْضِ فَانْظُروا كَيْفَ كانَ عاقِبَةُ الْمُكَذِّبِينَ (137) هذا بَيانٌ لِلنَّاسِ وَهُدىً وَمَوْعِظَةٌ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (138) وَلا تَهِنُوا وَلا تَحْزَنُوا وَأَنْتُمُ الْأَعْلَوْنَ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (139)\nقلت: السّنن: الطرق المسلوكة، وقيل: الأمم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قد مضت مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ سُنَنٌ جرت على الأمم المكذبة لأنبيائها قبلكم، وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لِسُنَّةِ اللَّهِ تَبْدِيلًا، وهو إمهالي واستدراجي إياهم، حتى يبلغ الكتاب الذي أجل لهم، فإذا بلغهم أهلكتهم، وأدلتُ الأنبياء وأتباعَهم عليهم، فإذا هلكوا بقيت آثارهم دارسة، اعتباراً لمن يأتي بعدهم، فَسِيرُوا فِي الْأَرْضِ وتعرفوا أخبارهم، وانظروا كَيْفَ كانَ عاقِبَةُ الْمُكَذِّبِينَ لأنبيائهم قبلكم، فكذلك يكون شأنكم مع مَنْ كذَّبكم.\nهذا الذي أمرتكم به من الاعتبار، بَيانٌ لِلنَّاسِ لمن أراد أن يعتبر من الكفار، وزيادة هداية واستبصار لِلْمُتَّقِينَ.\nثم سلاَّهم وبشرهم فقال: وَلا تَهِنُوا أي: لا تضعفوا عن قتال عدوكم بما أصابكم، وَلا تَحْزَنُوا على من قُتل منكم، وهم سبعون من الأنصار وخمسة من المهاجرين، منهم: حمزة بن عبد المطلب، ومصعب بن عمير- صاحب راية النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم وعبد الله بن جحش، وعثمان بن شماس، وسعد مولى عتبة- رضى الله عنهم-.\nأو: (لا تحزنوا) لفوات الغنيمة وَأَنْتُمُ الْأَعْلَوْنَ بأن تكون لكم العاقبة والنصر، أو: وحالكم أنكم أعلى منهم شأناً، فإنكم على الحق وقتالكم لله، وقتلاكم في الجنة، وهم على الباطل، وقتالهم للشيطان، وقتلاهم في النار، فلا تفشلوا عن الجهاد إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ فإن الإيمان يقتضي قوة القلب بالوثوق بالله والاعتماد عليه، أو: (إن كنتم مؤمنين) بما وعدتكم من العلو والنصر. والله أعلم.\nالإشارة: قد خلت من قبلكم، أيها المريدون، سنن الله في أوليائه مع المنكرين عليهم من عوام عباده، فإنه أبعدهم عن ساحة حضرته، وحرمهم من سابق عنايته، حتى ماتوا على البُعد، فاندرست آثارهم وخربت ديارهم، فسيروا في الأرض وانظروا كيف كان عاقبةُ المكذِّبين لأوليائه، هذا بيان للمعتبرين، وزيادة هدى وموعظة للمتقين، فلا تهنوا أيها الفقراء وتضعفوا عن طلب الحق بالرجوع عن طريق الجد والاجتهاد، لما يصيبكم من أذى أهل العناد، وأنتم الأعلون بالنصر والتأييد، ورفع درجاتكم مع خواص أهل التوحيد، إن كنتم مؤمنين بوعد الملك المجيد، فمن طلب الله وجده، وأنجز بالوفاء موعده، لكن بعد تجرع كؤوس مرارة الصبر، ودوام الحمد والشكر، وأنشدوا:\nلا تَحْسَب المجْد تَمْراً أَنْتَ آكِلُهُ ... لَنْ تَبْلُغَ المَجْدَ حَتَّى تَلْعَقَ الصّبر(1)\nثم سلاهم بمشاركة المكذبين فيما أصابهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"قَدْ خَلَتْ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ سُنَنٌ فَسِيرُوا فِي الْأَرْضِ فَانْظُروا كَيْفَ كانَ عاقِبَةُ الْمُكَذِّبِينَ (137) هذا بَيانٌ لِلنَّاسِ وَهُدىً وَمَوْعِظَةٌ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (138) وَلا تَهِنُوا وَلا تَحْزَنُوا وَأَنْتُمُ الْأَعْلَوْنَ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (139)","quran_en":"137. Ways of God have passed before you. So journey through the land and consider how the deniers came to their end. 138. This is a clear exposition for humankind, and guidance and an admonition for those mindful of God. 139. Do not lose heart and do not grieve: you shall be uppermost, if you are believers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Sunan (سُنَن) as paths and recurring precedents","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Sunan<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السُّنَن<\/bdi>) means paths that have been trodden; according to another explanation, it refers to the nations themselves. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N124\">[XIV-N124]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Sunan ( السُّنَن ) means paths that have been trodden; according to another explanation, it refers to the nations themselves. [XIV-N124]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The fate of those who deny and the patience of those who believe","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: Before you, patterns (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سُنَن<\/bdi>) took their course among peoples who denied their prophets, and “you shall never find any alteration in God’s pattern” [Q 33:62]. It belongs to His way that He gives them respite, leading them on by stages (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إمهال واستدراج<\/bdi>), until the appointed term He has written for them comes. Once it arrives, He destroys them and grants His prophets and their followers victory over them. After their destruction, their ruins remain as an admonition to those who follow. “Journey through the land” and discover their accounts; “consider how the deniers came to their end,” those who denied their prophets before you. The same will hold in your struggle with those who deny you.<\/p><p>“This”—the reflection I have commanded you to undertake—“is an exposition for humankind,” for those among the disbelievers who wish to draw a lesson; and it is still further “guidance and admonition for the mindful.”<\/p><p>Then He consoled the believers and gave them glad tidings: “Do not lose heart,” meaning do not become too weak to fight your enemy on account of what has happened to you; “and do not grieve” over those killed among you. Ibn ʿAjība counts seventy Helpers and five Emigrants, naming Ḥamza ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr (bearer of the Prophet’s standard), ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh, ʿUthmān ibn Shammās and Saʿd, the freedman of ʿUtba, may God be pleased with them. Alternatively, “do not grieve” over lost booty. “You shall be uppermost”: the final outcome and victory will be yours, or you are already above them in rank because you stand upon the truth and fight for God, your dead enter Paradise, while they stand upon falsehood and fight for Satan and their dead enter the Fire. Do not fail in your striving “if you are believers,” for faith entails a heart strengthened by trusting and relying upon God—or if you believe what I promised of elevation and victory. God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N125\">[XIV-N125]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: Before you, patterns ( سُنَن ) took their course among peoples who denied their prophets, and “you shall never find any alteration in God’s pattern” [Q 33:62]. It belongs to His way that He gives them respite, leading them on by stages ( إمهال واستدراج ), until the appointed term He has written for them comes. Once it arrives, He destroys them and grants His prophets and their followers victory over them. After their destruction, their ruins remain as an admonition to those who follow. “Journey through the land” and discover their accounts; “consider how the deniers came to their end,” those who denied their prophets before you. The same will hold in your struggle with those who deny you. “This”—the reflection I have commanded you to undertake—“is an exposition for humankind,” for those among the disbelievers who wish to draw a lesson; and it is still further “guidance and admonition for the mindful.” Then He consoled the believers and gave them glad tidings: “Do not lose heart,” meaning do not become too weak to fight your enemy on account of what has happened to you; “and do not grieve” over those killed among you. Ibn ʿAjība counts seventy Helpers and five Emigrants, naming Ḥamza ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr (bearer of the Prophet’s standard), ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh, ʿUthmān ibn Shammās and Saʿd, the freedman of ʿUtba, may God be pleased with them. Alternatively, “do not grieve” over lost booty. “You shall be uppermost”: the final outcome and victory will be yours, or you are already above them in rank because you stand upon the truth and fight for God, your dead enter Paradise, while they stand upon falsehood and fight for Satan and their dead enter the Fire. Do not fail in your striving “if you are believers,” for faith entails a heart strengthened by trusting and relying upon God—or if you believe what I promised of elevation and victory. God knows best. [XIV-N125]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The trials of the saints and the perseverance of disciples","html":"<p>Before you, O disciples (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريدون<\/bdi>), God’s established ways took effect among His friends (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أوليائه<\/bdi>) and those ordinary servants who rejected them. Such people were kept far from the court of His Presence and deprived of the solicitude He had previously offered, until they died at a distance, their traces disappearing and their dwellings falling to ruin. Travel the earth and look at the fate of those who denied His friends: this is an exposition for those willing to reflect, and added guidance and admonition for the mindful.<\/p><p>Do not grow weary, O spiritual poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>), or weaken in the quest for the Real by turning back from earnestness and effort because of injuries inflicted by obstinate people. You are the higher ones through divine help, reinforcement and the elevation of your ranks among the elect of the people of tawḥīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خواص أهل التوحيد<\/bdi>), if you truly believe the promise of the Glorious King. Whoever seeks God finds Him, and He fulfils what He has pledged—but only after one has drunk the bitter cups of patience and maintained praise and gratitude. They recite:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Do not suppose glory is a ripe date you merely eat;<br/>You shall not attain glory until you lick bitter patience.<\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"Before you, O disciples ( المريدون ), God’s established ways took effect among His friends ( أوليائه ) and those ordinary servants who rejected them. Such people were kept far from the court of His Presence and deprived of the solicitude He had previously offered, until they died at a distance, their traces disappearing and their dwellings falling to ruin. Travel the earth and look at the fate of those who denied His friends: this is an exposition for those willing to reflect, and added guidance and admonition for the mindful. Do not grow weary, O spiritual poor ( الفقراء ), or weaken in the quest for the Real by turning back from earnestness and effort because of injuries inflicted by obstinate people. You are the higher ones through divine help, reinforcement and the elevation of your ranks among the elect of the people of tawḥīd ( خواص أهل التوحيد ), if you truly believe the promise of the Glorious King. Whoever seeks God finds Him, and He fulfils what He has pledged—but only after one has drunk the bitter cups of patience and maintained praise and gratitude. They recite: Do not suppose glory is a ripe date you merely eat; You shall not attain glory until you lick bitter patience."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then consoled them by recalling that those who had denied them had likewise suffered injury.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then consoled them by recalling that those who had denied them had likewise suffered injury."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C412.1","page":412,"ar":"البيت للسلمي.","en":"The verse is by al-Sulamī.","anchor":"لَنْ تَبْلُغَ المَجْدَ حَتَّى تَلْعَقَ الصّبر"}],"english_characters":3392,"arabic_characters":2277},{"id":"q3-140-143","q":"3:140–143","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:140–143 · The alternation of fortune, testing and the meaning of perseverance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 412–414","cairo":"I:412–414","source_page_first":412,"source_page_last":414,"ar":"إِنْ يَمْسَسْكُمْ قَرْحٌ فَقَدْ مَسَّ الْقَوْمَ قَرْحٌ مِثْلُهُ وَتِلْكَ الْأَيَّامُ نُداوِلُها بَيْنَ النَّاسِ وَلِيَعْلَمَ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَيَتَّخِذَ مِنْكُمْ شُهَداءَ وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الظَّالِمِينَ (140) وَلِيُمَحِّصَ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَيَمْحَقَ الْكافِرِينَ (141) أَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ أَنْ تَدْخُلُوا الْجَنَّةَ وَلَمَّا يَعْلَمِ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ جاهَدُوا مِنْكُمْ وَيَعْلَمَ الصَّابِرِينَ (142) وَلَقَدْ كُنْتُمْ تَمَنَّوْنَ الْمَوْتَ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تَلْقَوْهُ فَقَدْ رَأَيْتُمُوهُ وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ (143)\nقلت: القرح- بالفتح والضم-: الجرح، وقيل: بالفتح: الجرح، وبالضم: ألمه ووجعه. والمداولة: المفاعلة من الدولة، وهي الغلبة، و (الأيام) : نعت أو خبر، و (نداولها) : خبر أو حال، و (ليعلم) : متعلق بمحذوف، أي: وفعل\nما فعل من الإدالة ليعلم، أو عطف على عِلة محذوفة، أي: نداولها ليكون كيت وكيت، وليعلم ... الخ، إيذاناً بأن العلة فيه غير واحدة، وأن ما يصيب المؤمن: فيه من المصالح ما لا يُعلم، و (يعلم الصابرين) : منصوب بأن، على أنَّ الواو للجمع.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنْ يَمْسَسْكُمْ في غزوة أحد قَرْحٌ كقتل أو جرح، فَقَدْ مَسَّ الْقَوْمَ من أعدائكم يوم بدر قَرْحٌ مِثْلُهُ، فإن كان قُتل منكم خمسة وسبعون يوم أحد، فقد قتل منهم يوم بدر سبعون وأسُرَ سبعون. أو: فَقَدْ مَسَّ الْقَوْمَ يوم أحد قَرْحٌ مثل ما أصابكم، فإنكم نلتم منهم وهزمتموهم، قبل أن تخالفوا أمر الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام-، كما نالوا منكم يومئذ. وَتِلْكَ الْأَيَّامُ نُداوِلُها بَيْنَ النَّاسِ أي: نُصرف دولتها بينهم، فنديل لهؤلاء تارة ولهؤلاء أخرى، كما قال الشاعر:\nفَيَوْمٌ عَلَيْنَا، ويَوْمٌ لنَا، ... ويَوْمٌ نُسَاءُ، ويَوْمٌ نُسَرْ(1)\nفقد أديل المسلمون على المشركين يوم بدر، فكانت الدولة لهم، وأديل المشركون يوم أحد. والمراد بالأيام: أيام الدنيا، أو أيام النصر والغلبة. وإنما أديل للمشركين يوم أحد ليتميز المؤمنون من المنافقين، ويظهر علمهم للناس، وليتخذ الله مِنْكُمْ شُهَداءَ حين ماتوا في الجهاد، أكرمهم الله بالشهادة، ولا تدل إدالة المشركين على أن الله يحبهم، فإن الله لا يُحِبُّ الظَّالِمِينَ. وإنما أدالهم لِيُمَحِّصَ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا أي: ليطهرهم ويصفيهم من الذنوب، وإنما أدال المسلمين على المشركين ليمحق الكافرين ويقطع دابرهم. والمحق: نقص الشيء قليلاً قليلاً.\nثم عاتب المسلمين فقال: أَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ أي: ظننتم أَنْ تَدْخُلُوا الْجَنَّةَ وَلَمَّا يَعْلَمِ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ جاهَدُوا مِنْكُمْ علم ظهوره، وَيَعْلَمَ الصَّابِرِينَ أي: لا تظنوا أن تدخلوا الجنة كما دخلها مَنْ قُتل منكم، ولم يقع منكم مثل ما وقع لهم من الجهاد والصبر على القتل والجرح حتى يقع العلم ظاهراً بجهادكم وصبركم.\nوَلَقَدْ كُنْتُمْ قبل خروجكم إلى الجهاد تَمَنَّوْنَ الْمَوْتَ أي: الحرب لأنه سبب الموت، وتقولون: ليت لنا يوماً مثل يوم بدر، فلقد لقيتموه وعاينتموه يوم أحد وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ من مات من إخوانكم، فما لكم حين رأيتموه جبنتم وانهزمتم؟ وهو عتاب لمن طلب الخروج يوم أحد، ثم انهزم عن الحرب، ثم تداركهم بالتوبة والعفو، على ما يأتي إن شاء الله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إن يمسسكم يا معشر الفقراء قرح كحبس أو ضرب أو سجن أو حَرج أو جلاء، فقد مس العموم مثل ذلك، غير أنكم تسيرون به إلى الله تعالى لمعرفتكم فيه، وهم لا سير لهم لعدم معرفتهم، أو إن يمسسكم قرح فقد مسَّ القوم المتقدمين من أهل الخصوصية مثل ما أصابكم، ففيهم أسوة لكم، وهذه عادة الله في أوليائه، يديل عليهم حتى يتطهروا ويتخلصوا، ثم يُديل لهم، وإنما أديل عليهم أولاً ليتطهروا من البقايا وتكمل فيهم المزايا، وليعلم\nالصادق في الطلب من الكاذب، فإن محبة الله مقرونة بالبلاء، وليتخذ منهم شهداء إن ماتوا على ذلك، كالحلاج وغيره، أو يتخذ منهم شهداء الملكوت إن صبروا حتى ظفروا بالشهود. (والله لا يحب الظالمين) أي: المؤذين لأوليائه، بل يمقتهم ويبعدهم.\n(وليمحص الله الذين آمنوا) بطريق الخصوص، أي: يخلصهم من بقايا الحس، سلط عليهم الناس، وليَمحق المنكرين عليهم بما يصيبهم من إيذايتهم، فإن المنكر على أهل النسبة كمن يدخل يده في الغيران(1) ، فإذا سلم من الأول والثاني، قال: لا يلحقني منهم شيء، فإذا أدخل يده في غار آخر لدغته حية فأهلكته.\nأم حسبتم يا معشر المريدين أن تدخلوا جنة المعارف، ولما يعلم الله الذين جاهدوا نفوسهم، ويعلم الصابرين على إيذاية من آذاهم، ولقد كنتم تمنون موت نفوسكم وتطلبون ما يعينكم على موتها من قبل أن تلقوا الجلال، فقد رأيتموه وعاينتموه وأنتم تنظرون ما أصاب الأولياء غيركم، فما لكم تجزعون منه وتفرون من مواطنه؟. وكان شيخ شيوخنا رضي الله عنه يقول: العجب كل العجب، ممن يطلب معرفة الله، فإذا تعرف إليه أنكره.\nوفي الحِكَم: «إذا فتح الله لك وجهة من التعرف فلا تبال معها، وإن قلَّ عملك، فإنه ما فتحها إلاَّ وهو يريد أن يتعرف إليك فيها، ألم تعلم أن التعرف هو مورده عليك، والأعمال أنت مهديها إليه، وأين ما تهديه إليه مما هو مورده عليك؟» . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم وبَّخهم على ما وقع لهم من الفشل، حين سمعوا بموت النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنْ يَمْسَسْكُمْ قَرْحٌ فَقَدْ مَسَّ الْقَوْمَ قَرْحٌ مِثْلُهُ وَتِلْكَ الْأَيَّامُ نُداوِلُها بَيْنَ النَّاسِ وَلِيَعْلَمَ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَيَتَّخِذَ مِنْكُمْ شُهَداءَ وَاللَّهُ لا يُحِبُّ الظَّالِمِينَ (140) وَلِيُمَحِّصَ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَيَمْحَقَ الْكافِرِينَ (141) أَمْ حَسِبْتُمْ أَنْ تَدْخُلُوا الْجَنَّةَ وَلَمَّا يَعْلَمِ اللَّهُ الَّذِينَ جاهَدُوا مِنْكُمْ وَيَعْلَمَ الصَّابِرِينَ (142) وَلَقَدْ كُنْتُمْ تَمَنَّوْنَ الْمَوْتَ مِنْ قَبْلِ أَنْ تَلْقَوْهُ فَقَدْ رَأَيْتُمُوهُ وَأَنْتُمْ تَنْظُرُونَ (143)","quran_en":"140. If a wound touches you, a like wound has already touched the other people. Such days We cause to alternate among humankind, so that God may make manifest those who believe and take witnesses from among you—and God does not love wrongdoers—141. and so that God may purify those who believe and wipe out the disbelievers. 142. Or did you imagine that you would enter the Garden before God had made manifest those among you who strove and made manifest those who were patient? 143. You used to wish for death before you met it; now you have seen it with your own eyes.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Qarḥ (قَرْح), alternation of victory and the syntax of purpose","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Qarḥ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القرح<\/bdi>), pronounced with fatḥa or ḍamma, means a wound. Alternatively, with fatḥa it is a wound and with ḍamma the pain and suffering of that wound. <strong>Mudāwala<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المداولة<\/bdi>) is the form expressing reciprocal action (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفاعلة<\/bdi>) from <strong>dawla<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دولة<\/bdi>), a turn of victory or mastery. “These days” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأيام<\/bdi>) may be an adjective (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعت<\/bdi>) or predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>), while “We cause them to alternate” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نداولها<\/bdi>) may be the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) or a circumstantial clause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>). “So that God may know” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وليعلم<\/bdi>) is attached to an omitted expression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محذوف<\/bdi>): “He did what He did in turning the fortunes of war so that He might know.” Or it may be coordinated with another omitted purpose: “We cause the days to alternate in order that such-and-such may happen, and that He might know ...,” indicating that there is more than one purpose and that believers’ sufferings involve benefits not fully known to us. “And know the patient” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ويعلم الصابرين<\/bdi>) is in the subjunctive (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منصوب<\/bdi>) through an understood <strong>an<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أن<\/bdi>), the <strong>wāw<\/strong> (و) expressing accompaniment: the two acts of knowing occur together. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N126\">[XIV-N126]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Qarḥ ( القرح ), pronounced with fatḥa or ḍamma, means a wound. Alternatively, with fatḥa it is a wound and with ḍamma the pain and suffering of that wound. Mudāwala ( المداولة ) is the form expressing reciprocal action ( مفاعلة ) from dawla ( دولة ), a turn of victory or mastery. “These days” ( الأيام ) may be an adjective ( نعت ) or predicate ( خبر ), while “We cause them to alternate” ( نداولها ) may be the predicate ( خبر ) or a circumstantial clause ( حال ). “So that God may know” ( وليعلم ) is attached to an omitted expression ( محذوف ): “He did what He did in turning the fortunes of war so that He might know.” Or it may be coordinated with another omitted purpose: “We cause the days to alternate in order that such-and-such may happen, and that He might know ...,” indicating that there is more than one purpose and that believers’ sufferings involve benefits not fully known to us. “And know the patient” ( ويعلم الصابرين ) is in the subjunctive ( منصوب ) through an understood an ( أن ), the wāw (و) expressing accompaniment: the two acts of knowing occur together. [XIV-N126]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The two battles and the alternating fortunes of nations","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “If a wound touches you” at Uḥud—whether through deaths or bodily injuries—“a similar wound has touched the other people,” your opponents, at Badr. If seventy-five of you were killed at Uḥud, seventy of them had been killed and seventy captured at Badr. Or it means that a similar injury touched them during Uḥud itself: at first you struck them and put them to flight, before disobeying the Messenger’s command, just as they later struck you. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N127\">[XIV-N127]<\/a><\/p><p>“These days We cause to alternate among humankind”: We turn the possession of victory among them, giving it at one time to these people and another time to those. As the poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">One day is against us, another day is ours;<br/>One day we suffer, another day we rejoice.<\/blockquote><p>The Muslims prevailed at Badr, and mastery was theirs; at Uḥud the idolaters prevailed. “Days” here may mean days of worldly life generally, or more particularly days of victory and mastery. God granted the idolaters a turn at Uḥud so that the believers might be distinguished from the hypocrites and their reality become publicly evident—“so that God may know those who believe,” in the sense of their state being manifested to people—and “take from among you witnesses” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهداء<\/bdi>), those who died in holy striving and were honoured with martyrdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهادة<\/bdi>). The idolaters’ temporary success does not prove divine love for them: “God does not love wrongdoers.” He permitted their advantage “so that God may purify those who believe,” cleansing and refining them of sins. In turn, He grants the believers power over their opponents “so that He may wipe out the disbelievers.” <strong>Maḥq<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحق<\/bdi>) means the gradual diminution of a thing until it disappears. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N128\">[XIV-N128]<\/a><\/p><p>Then He admonished the Muslims: “Or did you imagine that you would enter the Garden before God knew those who strove among you,” knowledge manifest in actual events (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم ظهوره<\/bdi>), “and knew those who were patient?” Do not imagine that you can enter Paradise in the way those killed among you have entered without displaying striving and steadfastness like theirs under killing and injury, until your striving and patience become apparent in the world.<\/p><p>“Before you went out to struggle, you used to wish for death,” meaning for battle, since battle can bring death. You would say, “If only we could have a day like Badr!” Now you have encountered what you wished for and seen it at Uḥud “while you looked on” as your brothers were killed. Why did some of you lose courage and flee when you saw it? This was a reproach of those who insisted upon going out at Uḥud but then retreated from combat. Yet God subsequently restored them through repentance and pardon, as will be explained, God willing. God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “If a wound touches you” at Uḥud—whether through deaths or bodily injuries—“a similar wound has touched the other people,” your opponents, at Badr. If seventy-five of you were killed at Uḥud, seventy of them had been killed and seventy captured at Badr. Or it means that a similar injury touched them during Uḥud itself: at first you struck them and put them to flight, before disobeying the Messenger’s command, just as they later struck you. [XIV-N127] “These days We cause to alternate among humankind”: We turn the possession of victory among them, giving it at one time to these people and another time to those. As the poet says: One day is against us, another day is ours; One day we suffer, another day we rejoice. The Muslims prevailed at Badr, and mastery was theirs; at Uḥud the idolaters prevailed. “Days” here may mean days of worldly life generally, or more particularly days of victory and mastery. God granted the idolaters a turn at Uḥud so that the believers might be distinguished from the hypocrites and their reality become publicly evident—“so that God may know those who believe,” in the sense of their state being manifested to people—and “take from among you witnesses” ( شهداء ), those who died in holy striving and were honoured with martyrdom ( شهادة ). The idolaters’ temporary success does not prove divine love for them: “God does not love wrongdoers.” He permitted their advantage “so that God may purify those who believe,” cleansing and refining them of sins. In turn, He grants the believers power over their opponents “so that He may wipe out the disbelievers.” Maḥq ( المحق ) means the gradual diminution of a thing until it disappears. [XIV-N128] Then He admonished the Muslims: “Or did you imagine that you would enter the Garden before God knew those who strove among you,” knowledge manifest in actual events ( علم ظهوره ), “and knew those who were patient?” Do not imagine that you can enter Paradise in the way those killed among you have entered without displaying striving and steadfastness like theirs under killing and injury, until your striving and patience become apparent in the world. “Before you went out to struggle, you used to wish for death,” meaning for battle, since battle can bring death. You would say, “If only we could have a day like Badr!” Now you have encountered what you wished for and seen it at Uḥud “while you looked on” as your brothers were killed. Why did some of you lose courage and flee when you saw it? This was a reproach of those who insisted upon going out at Uḥud but then retreated from combat. Yet God subsequently restored them through repentance and pardon, as will be explained, God willing. God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The testing of seekers as purification and disclosure","html":"<p>If a wound touches you, company of the spiritual poor, whether imprisonment, beating, detention, hardship or exile, a similar affliction has touched ordinary people. But through your knowledge of God within the affliction you advance toward Him, whereas they do not journey toward Him through it because they lack that knowledge. Or the verse can mean that a similar affliction touched earlier people of special spiritual realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الخصوصية<\/bdi>): their example should reassure you. This is God’s practice with His friends: He grants others the upper hand over them until they are cleansed and purified, and only thereafter grants them the upper hand. At first they are tested so that what remains of their attachments (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البقايا<\/bdi>) may be cleansed and their gifts completed; the sincere seeker is distinguished from the insincere, for love of God is accompanied by trial.<\/p><p>“He takes martyrs from among you” if they die in such tribulations, like al-Ḥallāj and others; or He takes witnesses of the Dominion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شهداء الملكوت<\/bdi>) from those who persevere until they attain direct witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود<\/bdi>). “God does not love wrongdoers,” those who injure His friends: rather, He detests them and sets them far away. “So that God may purify those who believe” along the path of special realization: He gives people power over them to cleanse them of the residues of sensory attachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بقايا الحس<\/bdi>). “And He may wipe out” those who reject them by reason of the wrongs they commit against them. The author compares someone who rejects the spiritually affiliated (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل النسبة<\/bdi>) to a person who thrusts his hand into successive caves. Having emerged safe from the first two, he says, “Nothing in them can harm me”; he then reaches into a third cave, where a snake bites and kills him. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N129\">[XIV-N129]<\/a><\/p><p>“Or did you think,” disciples, “that you would enter the Garden of direct knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جنة المعارف<\/bdi>) before God had made manifest those among you who struggle against their own souls (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جاهدوا نفوسهم<\/bdi>) and those who patiently endure others’ injuries?” You formerly desired the death of your ego-selves (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">موت نفوسكم<\/bdi>) and sought what might help to bring it about. Now you have met Majesty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجلال<\/bdi>), seen it with your own eyes and witnessed what befell other saints. Why then do you panic at it and flee the places where it appears? The shaykh of our shaykhs used to say: “How utterly astonishing is a person who asks for knowledge of God, yet when God makes Himself known to him, he rejects Him.”<\/p><p>In the <i>Ḥikam<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكم<\/bdi>) one reads: “When God opens for you a direction through which He makes Himself known (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وجهة من التعرف<\/bdi>), do not concern yourself if your work is slight. He opened it only because He wants to make Himself known to you through it. Do you not know that self-disclosure (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التعرف<\/bdi>) is what He brings to you, while works are what you offer Him? How incomparable are what you offer Him and what He brings to you!” And God grants success. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N130\">[XIV-N130]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"If a wound touches you, company of the spiritual poor, whether imprisonment, beating, detention, hardship or exile, a similar affliction has touched ordinary people. But through your knowledge of God within the affliction you advance toward Him, whereas they do not journey toward Him through it because they lack that knowledge. Or the verse can mean that a similar affliction touched earlier people of special spiritual realization ( أهل الخصوصية ): their example should reassure you. This is God’s practice with His friends: He grants others the upper hand over them until they are cleansed and purified, and only thereafter grants them the upper hand. At first they are tested so that what remains of their attachments ( البقايا ) may be cleansed and their gifts completed; the sincere seeker is distinguished from the insincere, for love of God is accompanied by trial. “He takes martyrs from among you” if they die in such tribulations, like al-Ḥallāj and others; or He takes witnesses of the Dominion ( شهداء الملكوت ) from those who persevere until they attain direct witnessing ( الشهود ). “God does not love wrongdoers,” those who injure His friends: rather, He detests them and sets them far away. “So that God may purify those who believe” along the path of special realization: He gives people power over them to cleanse them of the residues of sensory attachment ( بقايا الحس ). “And He may wipe out” those who reject them by reason of the wrongs they commit against them. The author compares someone who rejects the spiritually affiliated ( أهل النسبة ) to a person who thrusts his hand into successive caves. Having emerged safe from the first two, he says, “Nothing in them can harm me”; he then reaches into a third cave, where a snake bites and kills him. [XIV-N129] “Or did you think,” disciples, “that you would enter the Garden of direct knowledge ( جنة المعارف ) before God had made manifest those among you who struggle against their own souls ( جاهدوا نفوسهم ) and those who patiently endure others’ injuries?” You formerly desired the death of your ego-selves ( موت نفوسكم ) and sought what might help to bring it about. Now you have met Majesty ( الجلال ), seen it with your own eyes and witnessed what befell other saints. Why then do you panic at it and flee the places where it appears? The shaykh of our shaykhs used to say: “How utterly astonishing is a person who asks for knowledge of God, yet when God makes Himself known to him, he rejects Him.” In the Ḥikam ( الحكم ) one reads: “When God opens for you a direction through which He makes Himself known ( وجهة من التعرف ), do not concern yourself if your work is slight. He opened it only because He wants to make Himself known to you through it. Do you not know that self-disclosure ( التعرف ) is what He brings to you, while works are what you offer Him? How incomparable are what you offer Him and what He brings to you!” And God grants success. [XIV-N130]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then reproached them for the faltering that followed their hearing the news that the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, had died.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then reproached them for the faltering that followed their hearing the news that the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, had died."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C413.1","page":413,"ar":"البيت للنمر بن تولب، كما ورد في الكتاب لسيبويه 1/86.","en":"The verse is by al-Namir ibn Tawlab, as given in Sībawayh’s al-Kitāb, I:86.","anchor":"ويَوْمٌ نُسَرْ"},{"id":"C414.1","page":414,"ar":"الغيران: جمع غار، ويجمع أيضا على أغوار.","en":"Ghīrān is a plural of ghār (a cave or hole); it also has the plural aghwār.","anchor":"الغيران"}],"english_characters":6929,"arabic_characters":4372},{"id":"q3-144-145","q":"3:144–145","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:144–145 · The Prophet’s mortality, the field of Uḥud and dependence upon the One who does not die","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 414–416","cairo":"I:414–416","source_page_first":414,"source_page_last":416,"ar":"وَما مُحَمَّدٌ إِلاَّ رَسُولٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ الرُّسُلُ أَفَإِنْ ماتَ أَوْ قُتِلَ انْقَلَبْتُمْ عَلى أَعْقابِكُمْ وَمَنْ يَنْقَلِبْ عَلى عَقِبَيْهِ فَلَنْ يَضُرَّ اللَّهَ شَيْئاً وَسَيَجْزِي اللَّهُ الشَّاكِرِينَ (144) وَما كانَ لِنَفْسٍ أَنْ تَمُوتَ إِلاَّ بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ كِتاباً مُؤَجَّلاً وَمَنْ يُرِدْ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا نُؤْتِهِ مِنْها وَمَنْ يُرِدْ ثَوابَ الْآخِرَةِ نُؤْتِهِ مِنْها وَسَنَجْزِي الشَّاكِرِينَ (145)\nقلت: (كتاباً) : مصدر، أي: كتب الموت كتاباً مؤجلاً.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَما مُحَمَّدٌ إِلَّا رَسُولٌ يصيبه ما أصابهم، قَدْ مضت مِنْ قَبْلِهِ الرُّسُلُ، فسيمضي كما مضوا بالموت أو القتل، أَفَإِنْ ماتَ أَوْ قُتِلَ انْقَلَبْتُمْ عَلى أَعْقابِكُمْ بعد تقرر شريعته\nوظهور براهينه، عاتبهم على تقدير أن لو صار منهم انقلاب لو مات صلّى الله عليه وسلم أو قتل، أو على ما صدر من بعض المنافقين وهم ساكتون.\nقال أصحاب المغازي: خرج النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم حتى نزل بالشعب من أحُد، في سبعمائة رجل، وأمر عبد الله بن جبيرٍ على الرماة، وهم خمسون رجلاً، وقال: انضحوا عنا بالنبل، لا يأتونا من خلفنا، لا تبرحوا مكانكم كانت لنا أو علينا، فإنا لن نزال غالبين ما ثبتُّم مكانكم، فجاءت قريش، وعلى ميمنتهم خالد بن الوليد، وعلى ميسرتهم عكرمة، ومعهم النساء. ثم انتشب القتال فقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «مَنْ يأخذ هذا السيف بحقه» ؟ فجاء رجال فمنعهم، حتى جاء أبو دُجانة، فقال: وما حقه يا رسول الله؟ قال: «تضرب به العدو حتى ينحني» ، وكان رجلاً شجاعاً يختال عند الحرب، فأخذه واعتم بعمامة حمراء، وجعل يتبختر بين الصفين، فقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «إنها لمشيةٌ يبغضها الله إلاّ في هذا الموضع» .\nثم حمل النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم وأصحابه على المشركين فهزموهم، قال الزبير: (فرأيت هنداً وصواحبها هارباتٍ مصعدات في الجبل) ، فلما نظر الرماة إلى القوم قد انكشفوا، قالوا: الغنيمة الغنيمة فقال لهم بعضهم: لا تتركوا أمر النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فلم يلتفتوا، وانطلق عامتهم، فلما رأى خالد قلة الرماة، صاح في خيله من المشركين، ثم حمل على أصحاب النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم من خلفهم، وقتل عبد الله بن جبير، واختلط الناس، فقتل بعضُهم بعضاً، ورمى عبدُ الله بن قمئة الحارثي رسولَ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بحجر، فكسر أنفه ورباعيته، وشجَّه في وجهه، وكسر البيضة(1) على رأسه، فذبَّ عنه مصعبُ بن عمير، وكان صاحب الراية، فقتله ابن قمئة وهو يرى أنه قتل النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، فرجع إلى قومه، وقال: قد قتلت محمداً، وصرخ صارخ: ألا إنَّ محمداً قد مات. وقيل: إنه الشيطان، فانكفأ الناس، وجعل الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- يدعو: «إليَّ عباد الله» ، فانحاز إليه ثلاثون من الصحابة، وضموه حتى كشفوا عنه المشركين، وأصيبت يد طلحة بن عبيد الله فيبست، حين وقى بها النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، وأصيبت عين قتادة بن النعمان، حتى وقعت على وجنتيه، فردها النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم مكانها، فعادت أحسن مما كانت.\nوفشا في الناس أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم مات- فقال بعض المسلمين: ليت ابنُ أُبي يأخذ لنا أماناً من أبي سفيان.\nوقال بعض المنافقين: لو كان نبيّاً ما قتل، ارجعوا إلى دينكم الأول. فقال أنس بن النضر- عمُّ أنس بن مالك: (إن كان قد قتل محمدٌ فَإِنَّ رب محمد لا يموت، وما تصنعون بالحياة بعده؟ فقاتلوا على ما قاتل عليه، حتى تموتوا على ما مات عليه) . ثم قال: اللهم إني أعتذر إليك مما صنع هؤلاء- يعني المسلمين- وأبرأ إليك مما صنع هؤلاء- يعني الكفار-، ثم شدّ سيفه وقاتل حتى قُتل، رحمة الله عليه.\nفأنزل فيما قال المنافقون: وَمَنْ يَنْقَلِبْ عَلى عَقِبَيْهِ بارتداده فَلَنْ يَضُرَّ اللَّهَ شَيْئاً وإنما يضر نفسه، وَسَيَجْزِي اللَّهُ الشَّاكِرِينَ على نعمة الإسلام بالثبات عليه، كأنس وأضرابه، وَما كانَ ينبغي لِنَفْسٍ أَنْ تَمُوتَ إِلَّا بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ أي: بإرادته ومشيئته، أو بإذنه لملكٍ في قبض روحه، والمعنى: أنَّ لكل نفس أجلاً مسمى في علمه تعالى وقضائه، لا تستأخر عنه ساعة ولا تستقدم، بالتأخر عن القتال ولا بالإقدام عليه، وفيه تشجيعهم على القتال ووعد للرسول بحفظه وتأخر أجله فإن الله تعالى كتب أجل الموت كِتاباً مُؤَجَّلًا مؤقتاً لا يتقدم ولا يتأخر.\nونزل في الرماة الذين خالفوا المركز للغنيمة: وَمَنْ يُرِدْ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا نُؤْتِهِ مِنْها وَمَنْ يُرِدْ ثَوابَ الْآخِرَةِ نُؤْتِهِ مِنْها الجزاء الجليل، وَسَنَجْزِي الشَّاكِرِينَ الذين شكروا نعم الله، فلم يشغلهم شيء عن الجهاد في سبيل الله، بل كان همهم رضى الله ورسوله دون شيء سواه.\nالإشارة: ينبغي للمريد أن يستغني بالله، فلا يركن إلى شيء سواه، وتكون بصيرته نافذة حتى يغيب عن الواسطة بشهود الموسوط، فإن مات شيخه لم ينقلب على عقبيه، فإن تمكن من الشهود فقد استغنى عن كل موجود، وإن لم يتمكن نظر من يكمله، فالوقوف مع الوسائط وقوف مع النعم دون شهود المنعم، فلا يكون شاكراً للمْنعم حتى لا يحجبه عنه شيء، ولما مات- عليه الصلاة والسلام- دهشت الناس، وتحيّرت لوقوفهم مع شهود النعمة، إلاَّ الصدِّيق كان نفذ من شهود النعمة إلى شهود المُنعم، فخطب حينئذٍ على الناس، وقال: (مَنْ كانَ يَعْبُدُ مُحَمَّداً فإن مُحَمَّداً قَدْ ماتَ، ومَنْ كَانَ يَعْبُدُ اللَّهَ فإنَّ اللهَ حَيٌ لاَ يَمُوتِ) . ثم قرأ: وَما مُحَمَّدٌ إِلَّا رَسُولٌ ... إلى قوله:\nوَسَيَجْزِي الشَّاكِرِينَ، وهم الذين نفذوا إلى شهود المنعم، ولم يقفوا مع النعمة.\nودخل بعض العارفين على بعض الفقراء فوجده يبكي، فقال له: ما يبكيك؟ قال: مات أستاذي، فقال له العارف: ولم جعلت أستاذك يموت؟ وهلا جعلته حيّاً لا يموت. فنبهه على نفاذ بصيرته إلى شهود المنعم دون الوقوف مع النعمة، فالشيخ الحقيقي هو الذي يغني صاحبه عنه وعن غيره، بالدلالة على ربه.\nثم صبّرهم بما وقع لغيرهم قبلهم فقال:","quran_ar":"وَما مُحَمَّدٌ إِلاَّ رَسُولٌ قَدْ خَلَتْ مِنْ قَبْلِهِ الرُّسُلُ أَفَإِنْ ماتَ أَوْ قُتِلَ انْقَلَبْتُمْ عَلى أَعْقابِكُمْ وَمَنْ يَنْقَلِبْ عَلى عَقِبَيْهِ فَلَنْ يَضُرَّ اللَّهَ شَيْئاً وَسَيَجْزِي اللَّهُ الشَّاكِرِينَ (144) وَما كانَ لِنَفْسٍ أَنْ تَمُوتَ إِلاَّ بِإِذْنِ اللَّهِ كِتاباً مُؤَجَّلاً وَمَنْ يُرِدْ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا نُؤْتِهِ مِنْها وَمَنْ يُرِدْ ثَوابَ الْآخِرَةِ نُؤْتِهِ مِنْها وَسَنَجْزِي الشَّاكِرِينَ (145)","quran_en":"144. Muḥammad is no more than a messenger; messengers have passed away before him. If he should die or be killed, would you turn back upon your heels? Whoever turns back upon his heels cannot harm God in the least, and God will reward the thankful. 145. No soul can die except by God’s permission, at a term prescribed in writing. Whoever desires the reward of this world, We grant him some of it; whoever desires the reward of the Hereafter, We grant him some of it. We shall reward the thankful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Kitāban muʾajjalan (كِتاباً مُؤَجَّلاً)","html":"<p>I say: “In writing, at an appointed time” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كتاباً مؤجلاً<\/bdi>): <strong>kitāban<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كتاباً<\/bdi>) is a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/bdi>), meaning, “He wrote or decreed death as a writing whose time has been appointed.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N131\">[XIV-N131]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “In writing, at an appointed time” ( كتاباً مؤجلاً ): kitāban ( كتاباً ) is a verbal noun ( مصدر ), meaning, “He wrote or decreed death as a writing whose time has been appointed.” [XIV-N131]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"A report of death does not abolish the message","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Muḥammad is only a messenger,” susceptible to what befell the messengers before him: “Messengers have passed away before him”; he too shall pass away as they did, through death or killing. “If he dies or is killed, will you turn back upon your heels,” after his revealed Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شريعة<\/bdi>) has been established and his proofs made manifest? God rebukes them for the possibility of an actual reversal should the Prophet die or be killed—or for the remarks already made by certain hypocrites while the others remained silent. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N132\">[XIV-N132]<\/a><\/p><p>According to the authors of the military narratives (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أصحاب المغازي<\/bdi>), the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, marched to the ravine of Uḥud with seven hundred men. He appointed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr over fifty archers and instructed them: “Drive the attackers away with arrows so that they do not come upon us from behind. Do not leave your post whether we win or lose, for we shall remain victorious as long as you stay in your positions.” The Quraysh arrived, Khālid ibn al-Walīd on their right wing and ʿIkrima on their left, with women accompanying the forces. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N133\">[XIV-N133]<\/a><\/p><p>When the fighting began the Prophet asked: “Who will take this sword and discharge its right?” A number of men came, but he withheld it until Abū Dujāna approached. “What is its right, Messenger of God?” he asked. He answered: “That you strike the enemy with it until it bends.” Abū Dujāna was courageous and moved proudly in battle. Taking the sword, he wrapped a red turban around his head and strode between the two armies with a swagger. The Prophet said: “God hates such a way of walking, except in this place.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N133\">[XIV-N133]<\/a><\/p><p>The Prophet and his Companions then charged the idolaters and put them to flight. Al-Zubayr said: “I saw Hind and her companions fleeing up the mountain.” When the archers saw the enemy retreat, they cried, “The spoils! The spoils!” Some among them warned: “Do not abandon the Prophet’s instruction!” Most paid no heed and left. Seeing that few archers remained, Khālid called his cavalry of the idolaters, charged the Muslims from the rear and killed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr. Men became entangled in the confusion and some killed others of their own side.<\/p><p>ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qamiʾa al-Ḥārithī struck the Prophet with a stone, breaking his nose and an incisor, wounding his face and breaking the helmet upon his head. Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, who carried the standard, defended him, but Ibn Qamiʾa killed Muṣʿab believing he had killed the Prophet. He returned to his people crying, “I have killed Muḥammad.” Another cry spread: “Muḥammad has died!” Some said the cry came from Satan. The Muslim fighters fell back. The Messenger called, “Come to me, servants of God!” Thirty Companions rallied around him, formed a protective ring and drove off the idolaters. Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbayd Allāh’s hand was wounded and became paralysed while protecting him; Qatāda ibn al-Nuʿmān’s eye was struck so that it fell upon his cheek. The source relates that the Prophet restored it to its place and it became better than before. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N134\">[XIV-N134]<\/a><\/p><p>The rumour of the Prophet’s death spread. Some Muslims said, “If only Ibn Ubayy could obtain for us a guarantee of safety from Abū Sufyān.” Some hypocrites said: “Had he been a prophet, he would not have been killed. Return to your former religion.” Anas ibn al-Naḍr, the uncle of Anas ibn Mālik, replied: “If Muḥammad has been killed, the Lord of Muḥammad does not die. What would you do with life after him? Fight for what he fought for, until you die upon what he died upon.” He then prayed: “God, I offer my apology for what these”—meaning the Muslims—“have done, and I dissociate myself before You from what these”—meaning the unbelievers—“have done.” He took up his sword and fought until killed. May God show him mercy. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N135\">[XIV-N135]<\/a><\/p><p>Concerning the hypocrites’ speech God revealed: “Whoever turns back upon his heels,” by abandoning his religion, “will not harm God in the least,” but will only injure himself. “God will reward the thankful,” those who thank Him for the gift of Islam by remaining steadfast, as Anas ibn al-Naḍr and others like him did.<\/p><p>“No soul can die except by God’s permission”: through His will and volition, or through His permission to an angel charged with taking that soul. Every soul has a named term within God’s knowledge and decree; it cannot be delayed by an hour nor brought forward, either by holding back from battle or by plunging into it. This encourages steadfastness in fighting and promises preservation of the Messenger until his appointed term has elapsed. God has written death “as a writing with an appointed time”: its term neither advances nor retreats. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N136\">[XIV-N136]<\/a><\/p><p>Concerning the archers who forsook their assigned position for the sake of booty, God revealed: “Whoever desires the reward of this world, We give him some of it; and whoever desires the reward of the Hereafter, We give him some of it,” a noble recompense. “We will reward the thankful”: those who truly thank God for His gifts, letting nothing divert them from striving in His path and desiring nothing but the pleasure of God and His Messenger.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “Muḥammad is only a messenger,” susceptible to what befell the messengers before him: “Messengers have passed away before him”; he too shall pass away as they did, through death or killing. “If he dies or is killed, will you turn back upon your heels,” after his revealed Law ( شريعة ) has been established and his proofs made manifest? God rebukes them for the possibility of an actual reversal should the Prophet die or be killed—or for the remarks already made by certain hypocrites while the others remained silent. [XIV-N132] According to the authors of the military narratives ( أصحاب المغازي ), the Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, marched to the ravine of Uḥud with seven hundred men. He appointed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr over fifty archers and instructed them: “Drive the attackers away with arrows so that they do not come upon us from behind. Do not leave your post whether we win or lose, for we shall remain victorious as long as you stay in your positions.” The Quraysh arrived, Khālid ibn al-Walīd on their right wing and ʿIkrima on their left, with women accompanying the forces. [XIV-N133] When the fighting began the Prophet asked: “Who will take this sword and discharge its right?” A number of men came, but he withheld it until Abū Dujāna approached. “What is its right, Messenger of God?” he asked. He answered: “That you strike the enemy with it until it bends.” Abū Dujāna was courageous and moved proudly in battle. Taking the sword, he wrapped a red turban around his head and strode between the two armies with a swagger. The Prophet said: “God hates such a way of walking, except in this place.” [XIV-N133] The Prophet and his Companions then charged the idolaters and put them to flight. Al-Zubayr said: “I saw Hind and her companions fleeing up the mountain.” When the archers saw the enemy retreat, they cried, “The spoils! The spoils!” Some among them warned: “Do not abandon the Prophet’s instruction!” Most paid no heed and left. Seeing that few archers remained, Khālid called his cavalry of the idolaters, charged the Muslims from the rear and killed ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr. Men became entangled in the confusion and some killed others of their own side. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qamiʾa al-Ḥārithī struck the Prophet with a stone, breaking his nose and an incisor, wounding his face and breaking the helmet upon his head. Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, who carried the standard, defended him, but Ibn Qamiʾa killed Muṣʿab believing he had killed the Prophet. He returned to his people crying, “I have killed Muḥammad.” Another cry spread: “Muḥammad has died!” Some said the cry came from Satan. The Muslim fighters fell back. The Messenger called, “Come to me, servants of God!” Thirty Companions rallied around him, formed a protective ring and drove off the idolaters. Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbayd Allāh’s hand was wounded and became paralysed while protecting him; Qatāda ibn al-Nuʿmān’s eye was struck so that it fell upon his cheek. The source relates that the Prophet restored it to its place and it became better than before. [XIV-N134] The rumour of the Prophet’s death spread. Some Muslims said, “If only Ibn Ubayy could obtain for us a guarantee of safety from Abū Sufyān.” Some hypocrites said: “Had he been a prophet, he would not have been killed. Return to your former religion.” Anas ibn al-Naḍr, the uncle of Anas ibn Mālik, replied: “If Muḥammad has been killed, the Lord of Muḥammad does not die. What would you do with life after him? Fight for what he fought for, until you die upon what he died upon.” He then prayed: “God, I offer my apology for what these”—meaning the Muslims—“have done, and I dissociate myself before You from what these”—meaning the unbelievers—“have done.” He took up his sword and fought until killed. May God show him mercy. [XIV-N135] Concerning the hypocrites’ speech God revealed: “Whoever turns back upon his heels,” by abandoning his religion, “will not harm God in the least,” but will only injure himself. “God will reward the thankful,” those who thank Him for the gift of Islam by remaining steadfast, as Anas ibn al-Naḍr and others like him did. “No soul can die except by God’s permission”: through His will and volition, or through His permission to an angel charged with taking that soul. Every soul has a named term within God’s knowledge and decree; it cannot be delayed by an hour nor brought forward, either by holding back from battle or by plunging into it. This encourages steadfastness in fighting and promises preservation of the Messenger until his appointed term has elapsed. God has written death “as a writing with an appointed time”: its term neither advances nor retreats. [XIV-N136] Concerning the archers who forsook their assigned position for the sake of booty, God revealed: “Whoever desires the reward of this world, We give him some of it; and whoever desires the reward of the Hereafter, We give him some of it,” a noble recompense. “We will reward the thankful”: those who truly thank God for His gifts, letting nothing divert them from striving in His path and desiring nothing but the pleasure of God and His Messenger."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The death of a master and the independence of the disciple’s vision","html":"<p>The disciple should seek sufficiency in God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستغناء بالله<\/bdi>), resting upon none other than Him. His spiritual insight (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بصيرة<\/bdi>) should penetrate so deeply that he is absent to the intermediary (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواسطة<\/bdi>) through witnessing the One mediated (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الموسوط<\/bdi>). If his spiritual master dies, he will not turn back upon his heels. If he is firmly established in witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشهود<\/bdi>), he has become independent of every created existent. If he has not attained establishment, he seeks someone who can bring him to completion. To remain fixed on the intermediaries is to remain at the gifts without witnessing the Giver (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المنعم<\/bdi>). He is not fully thankful to the Giver until nothing can veil Him from him. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N137\">[XIV-N137]<\/a><\/p><p>When the Prophet died, people were stunned and bewildered because they stood at the gift and what they had witnessed of it. The Truthful One (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصدِّيق<\/bdi>), however, had passed beyond witnessing the gift to witnessing the Giver. He addressed the people, saying: “Whoever worshipped Muḥammad should know that Muḥammad has died; whoever worships God should know that God is Ever-Living and does not die.” He then recited: “Muḥammad is only a messenger ...” down to “He shall reward the thankful.” These are those who pass through to the witnessing of the Giver and do not remain with the gift. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N138\">[XIV-N138]<\/a><\/p><p>One of the knowers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العارفين<\/bdi>) visited a spiritual poor man (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فقير<\/bdi>) and found him weeping. “Why are you weeping?” he asked. “My teacher has died,” the man replied. The knower asked: “Why did you make your teacher one who dies? Why not make your teacher the Living One who does not die?” He awakened him to the need to penetrate through to the Giver rather than stopping at the gift. For the authentic spiritual teacher is the one who makes his companion independent of the teacher himself and of all others by directing him to his Lord.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The disciple should seek sufficiency in God ( الاستغناء بالله ), resting upon none other than Him. His spiritual insight ( بصيرة ) should penetrate so deeply that he is absent to the intermediary ( الواسطة ) through witnessing the One mediated ( الموسوط ). If his spiritual master dies, he will not turn back upon his heels. If he is firmly established in witnessing ( الشهود ), he has become independent of every created existent. If he has not attained establishment, he seeks someone who can bring him to completion. To remain fixed on the intermediaries is to remain at the gifts without witnessing the Giver ( المنعم ). He is not fully thankful to the Giver until nothing can veil Him from him. [XIV-N137] When the Prophet died, people were stunned and bewildered because they stood at the gift and what they had witnessed of it. The Truthful One ( الصدِّيق ), however, had passed beyond witnessing the gift to witnessing the Giver. He addressed the people, saying: “Whoever worshipped Muḥammad should know that Muḥammad has died; whoever worships God should know that God is Ever-Living and does not die.” He then recited: “Muḥammad is only a messenger ...” down to “He shall reward the thankful.” These are those who pass through to the witnessing of the Giver and do not remain with the gift. [XIV-N138] One of the knowers ( العارفين ) visited a spiritual poor man ( فقير ) and found him weeping. “Why are you weeping?” he asked. “My teacher has died,” the man replied. The knower asked: “Why did you make your teacher one who dies? Why not make your teacher the Living One who does not die?” He awakened him to the need to penetrate through to the Giver rather than stopping at the gift. For the authentic spiritual teacher is the one who makes his companion independent of the teacher himself and of all others by directing him to his Lord."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then called them to patience through the example of what earlier followers had endured.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then called them to patience through the example of what earlier followers had endured."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C415.1","page":415,"ar":"البيضة: الخوذة.","en":"Bayḍa: a helmet.","anchor":"وكسر البيضة"}],"english_characters":7290,"arabic_characters":4909},{"id":"q3-146-148","q":"3:146–148","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:146–148 · The ribbiyyūn: steadfast followers, devotion after losing one’s teacher and competing Qurʾānic readings","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 416–418","cairo":"I:416–418","source_page_first":416,"source_page_last":418,"ar":"وَكَأَيِّنْ مِنْ نَبِيٍّ قُتِلَ مَعَهُ رِبِّيُّونَ كَثِيرٌ فَما وَهَنُوا لِما أَصابَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَما ضَعُفُوا وَمَا اسْتَكانُوا وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الصَّابِرِينَ (146) وَما كانَ قَوْلَهُمْ إِلاَّ أَنْ قالُوا رَبَّنَا اغْفِرْ لَنا ذُنُوبَنا وَإِسْرافَنا فِي أَمْرِنا وَثَبِّتْ أَقْدامَنا وَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ (147) فَآتاهُمُ اللَّهُ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا وَحُسْنَ ثَوابِ الْآخِرَةِ وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (148)\nقلت: (كأَيِّن) : أصله: أيْ، دخلت الكاف عليها وصارت بمعنى (كم) ، وأثبت التنوين نوناً على غير قياس، وقرأ ابن كثير: (وكائن) ، على وزن فاعل، ووجهه: أنه قلب الياء قبل الهمزة فصار: كيَاءٍ، تحركت الياء وانفتح ما قبلها فصار كائن، وهما لغتان، وقد جمع الشاعر بينهما في بيت، فقال:\nكَأيّنْ أبَدْنَا مِنْ عَدوٍّ بعِزِّنا ... وكَائِنْ أجَرْنا مِنْ ضَعيفٍ وخائِفِ\nو (الرِبِّيون) : جمع رُبَّة، أي: الفرقة. أي: معه جموع كثيرة، وقيل: العلماء الأتقياء، وقيل: الولاة، وهو: إما مبتدأ فيوقف على (قُتل) ، أو نائب فاعل (قُتل) ، أو فاعل على من قرأ بالبناء له، و (كثير) : نعت له، كقوله:\nوَالْمَلائِكَةُ بَعْدَ ذلِكَ ظَهِيرٌ لأن فعيلاً يخبر به عن المفرد والجمع.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَكَأَيِّنْ وكم مِنْ نَبِيٍّ قُتِلَ في المعركة ومعه جموع كثيرة، أو ربانيون علماء أتقياء، فلم يفشلوا ولم يضعفوا، بل ثبتوا على دينهم وجهاد عدوهم، أو يقول: كثير من الأنبياء قتل معهم ربانيون كثير، أي: ماتوا في الحرب فثبت الباقون، ولم يفتروا ولم يضعفوا عن عدوهم، ويترجح الأول بما صرَخَ به الصارخ يوم أحد: إن محمدا قد مات، فضرب لهم المثل بقوله: وَكَأَيِّنْ مِنْ نَبِيٍّ قُتِلَ، ويترجح الثاني بأنه لم يقتل نبيّ قط في المحاربة.\nأو: وَكَأَيِّنْ مِنْ نَبِيٍّ قاتَلَ أي: جاهد معه رِبِّيُّونَ كَثِيرٌ، وبعد ما قتل نبيهم أو جموعهم فَما وَهَنُوا لِما أَصابَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أي: فما فتروا، ولم ينكسر جندهم لأجل ما أصابهم من قتل نبيهم أو بعضهم، وَما ضَعُفُوا عن جهاد عدوهم ولا عن دينهم، وَمَا اسْتَكانُوا أي: خضعوا لعدوهم، من السكون لأن الخاضع يسكن لعدوه يفعل به ما يريد، فالألف إشباع زائد، أي: فما سكنوا لعدوهم بل صبروا له، وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الصَّابِرِينَ فينصرهم ويعزهم ويُعظم قدرهم.\nوَما كانَ قَوْلَهُمْ عند قتل نبيهم مع ثباتهم على دينه، إِلَّا أَنْ قالُوا رَبَّنَا اغْفِرْ لَنا ذُنُوبَنا الصغائر، وَإِسْرافَنا فِي أَمْرِنا أي: ما تجاوزنا به الحد في أمر ذنوبنا، كالكبائر، وَثَبِّتْ أَقْدامَنا في مداحض الحرب لئلا ننهزم، وَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ من أعدائنا، فَهلاَّ فعلتم مثلهم، وقلتم ذلك يا أصحاب محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم.\nفَآتاهُمُ اللَّهُ في ثواب الاستغفار واللجوء إلى الله ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا وهو النصر والغنيمة والعز وحسن الذكر، وَحُسْنَ ثَوابِ الْآخِرَةِ وهو النعيم الذي لا يفنى ولا يبيد، وخص ثواب الآخرة بالحسن إشعاراً بفضله، وأنه المعتد به عنده، وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ الثابتين على دينهم، لأنهم أحسنوا فيما بينهم وبين ربهم بحفظ دينه، فأحبهم الله وقربهم إلى حضرته.\nالإشارة: وكم من المريدين والأتباع مات شيخهم أو قتل، فثبتوا على طريقهم، فما فَشِلوا ولا ضعفوا، ولا خضعوا لمن يقطعهم عن ربهم، بل صبروا على السير إلى ربهم، أو الترقي في المقامات، ومن لم يرشد منهم طلب من يكمل له، (والله يحب الصابرين) ، فإذا أحبهم كان سمعهم وبصرهم، كما في الحديث. وما كان حالهم عند موت شيخهم إلا الالتجاء إلى ربهم، والاستغفار مما بقي من مساوئهم، وطلب الثبات في مواطن حرب أنفسهم، فأعطاهم الله عزّ الدنيا والآخرة، عزّ الدنيا بالإيمان والمعرفة، وعزّ الآخرة بدوام المشاهدة، فكانوا أحباب الله (والله يحب المحسنين) .\nثم حذّرهم الله تعالى من الركون إلى عدوهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَكَأَيِّنْ مِنْ نَبِيٍّ قُتِلَ مَعَهُ رِبِّيُّونَ كَثِيرٌ فَما وَهَنُوا لِما أَصابَهُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ وَما ضَعُفُوا وَمَا اسْتَكانُوا وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الصَّابِرِينَ (146) وَما كانَ قَوْلَهُمْ إِلاَّ أَنْ قالُوا رَبَّنَا اغْفِرْ لَنا ذُنُوبَنا وَإِسْرافَنا فِي أَمْرِنا وَثَبِّتْ أَقْدامَنا وَانْصُرْنا عَلَى الْقَوْمِ الْكافِرِينَ (147) فَآتاهُمُ اللَّهُ ثَوابَ الدُّنْيا وَحُسْنَ ثَوابِ الْآخِرَةِ وَاللَّهُ يُحِبُّ الْمُحْسِنِينَ (148)","quran_en":"146. How many a prophet was killed while many devoted followers were with him; they did not lose heart because of what befell them in God’s path, nor weaken, nor submit in abasement. God loves those who are patient. 147. Their words were nothing but: “Our Lord, forgive us our sins and our excess in our affairs, make our feet firm, and help us against the disbelieving people.” 148. God gave them the reward of this world and the excellent reward of the Hereafter; and God loves those who do beautiful good.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Kaʾayyin (كَأَيِّن), ribbiyyūn (رِبِّيُّون), and qātala/qutila","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Kaʾayyin<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كَأَيِّن<\/bdi>) originally consisted of <strong>ayy<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أي<\/bdi>) preceded by <strong>kāf<\/strong> (ك), becoming a word meaning “how many” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كم<\/bdi>). Its tanwīn was established as a nūn in a way that does not follow the usual analogy (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على غير قياس<\/bdi>). Ibn Kathīr’s reading is <strong>kāʾin<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وكائن<\/bdi>), on the pattern of an active participle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاعل<\/bdi>). It can be explained by metathesis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قلب<\/bdi>), the yāʾ coming before the hamza, yielding <strong>kayāʾ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كياء<\/bdi>); when the yāʾ acquired a vowel and the preceding letter had fatḥa, it became <strong>kāʾin<\/strong>. These are two attested linguistic forms. One poet combined them in a single verse:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">How many an enemy (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كَأَيِّن<\/bdi>) we destroyed through our might,<br/>And how many a weak and fearful person (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كَائِن<\/bdi>) we protected.<\/blockquote><p><strong>Ribbiyyūn<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرِبِّيُّون<\/bdi>) is explained as the plural of <strong>rubba<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رُبَّة<\/bdi>), meaning a company or group; hence “many companies fought with him.” Other explanations make the word “devout, Godfearing scholars” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلماء الأتقياء<\/bdi>), or rulers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاة<\/bdi>). The noun may be the subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/bdi>) of a new clause if one stops at a reading meaning “was killed” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قُتِل<\/bdi>), or it may be the passive subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نائب فاعل<\/bdi>) of that form, or the agent/subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاعل<\/bdi>) if one reads the verb in the active (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بالبناء للفاعل<\/bdi>). “Many” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كثير<\/bdi>) qualifies <strong>ribbiyyūn<\/strong>, as in “and the angels, after that, are supporters” [Q 66:4]; a word on the pattern <strong>faʿīl<\/strong> can be predicated of singulars and plurals alike. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N139\">[XIV-N139]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Kaʾayyin ( كَأَيِّن ) originally consisted of ayy ( أي ) preceded by kāf (ك), becoming a word meaning “how many” ( كم ). Its tanwīn was established as a nūn in a way that does not follow the usual analogy ( على غير قياس ). Ibn Kathīr’s reading is kāʾin ( وكائن ), on the pattern of an active participle ( فاعل ). It can be explained by metathesis ( قلب ), the yāʾ coming before the hamza, yielding kayāʾ ( كياء ); when the yāʾ acquired a vowel and the preceding letter had fatḥa, it became kāʾin . These are two attested linguistic forms. One poet combined them in a single verse: How many an enemy ( كَأَيِّن ) we destroyed through our might, And how many a weak and fearful person ( كَائِن ) we protected. Ribbiyyūn ( الرِبِّيُّون ) is explained as the plural of rubba ( رُبَّة ), meaning a company or group; hence “many companies fought with him.” Other explanations make the word “devout, Godfearing scholars” ( العلماء الأتقياء ), or rulers ( الولاة ). The noun may be the subject ( مبتدأ ) of a new clause if one stops at a reading meaning “was killed” ( قُتِل ), or it may be the passive subject ( نائب فاعل ) of that form, or the agent/subject ( فاعل ) if one reads the verb in the active ( بالبناء للفاعل ). “Many” ( كثير ) qualifies ribbiyyūn , as in “and the angels, after that, are supporters” [Q 66:4]; a word on the pattern faʿīl can be predicated of singulars and plurals alike. [XIV-N139]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The followers of former prophets","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “How many a prophet was killed” in battle, with great companies or Godfearing scholars accompanying him, and they did not fail or grow weak. Rather, they stood firm in their faith and in their struggle against the enemy. Or the verse may be understood as: how many prophets had numerous devoted followers who were killed fighting alongside them? The survivors remained steadfast and did not grow languid or weak before the enemy. The first interpretation is strengthened by the cry heard at Uḥud that Muḥammad had died, for God set before them an exemplary contrast through “how many a prophet was killed”; the other is said to gain support from the assertion that no prophet was ever killed in open battle. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N140\">[XIV-N140]<\/a><\/p><p>Alternatively, “How many a prophet fought” means that many devoted followers struggled alongside him; even after their prophet or some of their own number were killed, “they did not lose heart because of what befell them in God’s path.” They did not slacken or lose cohesion because of their prophet’s death or the killing of some of their men; “nor did they weaken” in fighting or adhering to their religion; “nor did they submit,” meaning yield to their enemy. Ibn ʿAjība connects <strong>istakānū<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استكانوا<\/bdi>) with <strong>sukūn<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السكون<\/bdi>), the stillness of someone who submits to an opponent and lets that opponent do whatever he wishes, regarding the alif as an added prolongation. Rather, they persevered, and “God loves the patient,” granting them victory, dignity and exalted standing.<\/p><p>“Their words,” when their prophet was killed and they had remained firm in his religion, “were nothing but: ‘Our Lord, forgive us our sins,’” meaning lesser sins, “and our excess in our affair,” the ways in which they exceeded the bounds through graver sins; “and make our feet firm” where footing slips in battle so that we do not flee; “and grant us victory over the disbelieving people,” our enemies. Why did you not do as they did and pray thus, O Companions of Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him?<\/p><p>“God gave them,” in recompense for seeking forgiveness and seeking refuge in Him, “the reward of this world”: victory, booty, honour and good repute; and “the excellent reward of the Hereafter,” everlasting bliss. The latter reward is specially called excellent to mark its superiority and the weight it carries with Him. “God loves those who do beautiful good (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحسنين<\/bdi>),” those who stand firm in their religion, for they treated their Lord well by safeguarding His religion, and God therefore loved them and brought them near to His Presence.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “How many a prophet was killed” in battle, with great companies or Godfearing scholars accompanying him, and they did not fail or grow weak. Rather, they stood firm in their faith and in their struggle against the enemy. Or the verse may be understood as: how many prophets had numerous devoted followers who were killed fighting alongside them? The survivors remained steadfast and did not grow languid or weak before the enemy. The first interpretation is strengthened by the cry heard at Uḥud that Muḥammad had died, for God set before them an exemplary contrast through “how many a prophet was killed”; the other is said to gain support from the assertion that no prophet was ever killed in open battle. [XIV-N140] Alternatively, “How many a prophet fought” means that many devoted followers struggled alongside him; even after their prophet or some of their own number were killed, “they did not lose heart because of what befell them in God’s path.” They did not slacken or lose cohesion because of their prophet’s death or the killing of some of their men; “nor did they weaken” in fighting or adhering to their religion; “nor did they submit,” meaning yield to their enemy. Ibn ʿAjība connects istakānū ( استكانوا ) with sukūn ( السكون ), the stillness of someone who submits to an opponent and lets that opponent do whatever he wishes, regarding the alif as an added prolongation. Rather, they persevered, and “God loves the patient,” granting them victory, dignity and exalted standing. “Their words,” when their prophet was killed and they had remained firm in his religion, “were nothing but: ‘Our Lord, forgive us our sins,’” meaning lesser sins, “and our excess in our affair,” the ways in which they exceeded the bounds through graver sins; “and make our feet firm” where footing slips in battle so that we do not flee; “and grant us victory over the disbelieving people,” our enemies. Why did you not do as they did and pray thus, O Companions of Muḥammad, blessings and peace be upon him? “God gave them,” in recompense for seeking forgiveness and seeking refuge in Him, “the reward of this world”: victory, booty, honour and good repute; and “the excellent reward of the Hereafter,” everlasting bliss. The latter reward is specially called excellent to mark its superiority and the weight it carries with Him. “God loves those who do beautiful good ( المحسنين ),” those who stand firm in their religion, for they treated their Lord well by safeguarding His religion, and God therefore loved them and brought them near to His Presence."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"A disciple’s steadfastness after the death of a master","html":"<p>How many disciples and followers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريدين والأتباع<\/bdi>) have had their shaykh die or be killed, yet held fast to their path! They neither lost heart nor weakened nor submitted to those who would sever them from their Lord. Instead they patiently persevered in the journey toward God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السير إلى ربهم<\/bdi>) or in the ascent through stations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الترقي في المقامات<\/bdi>). Whoever had not yet received complete guidance sought another to complete it. “God loves the patient”; and when He loves them, He becomes their hearing and sight, as the hadith puts it. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N141\">[XIV-N141]<\/a><\/p><p>On losing their shaykh they had no recourse but to turn toward their Lord, seek forgiveness for the faults still remaining in them, and ask to be made firm upon the battlegrounds where they fought their own souls. God accordingly gave them honour in both abodes: honour in this world through faith and gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإيمان والمعرفة<\/bdi>), and honour in the next through perpetual witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">دوام المشاهدة<\/bdi>). Thus they were among God’s beloved: “God loves those who do beautiful good.”<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"How many disciples and followers ( المريدين والأتباع ) have had their shaykh die or be killed, yet held fast to their path! They neither lost heart nor weakened nor submitted to those who would sever them from their Lord. Instead they patiently persevered in the journey toward God ( السير إلى ربهم ) or in the ascent through stations ( الترقي في المقامات ). Whoever had not yet received complete guidance sought another to complete it. “God loves the patient”; and when He loves them, He becomes their hearing and sight, as the hadith puts it. [XIV-N141] On losing their shaykh they had no recourse but to turn toward their Lord, seek forgiveness for the faults still remaining in them, and ask to be made firm upon the battlegrounds where they fought their own souls. God accordingly gave them honour in both abodes: honour in this world through faith and gnosis ( الإيمان والمعرفة ), and honour in the next through perpetual witnessing ( دوام المشاهدة ). Thus they were among God’s beloved: “God loves those who do beautiful good.”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>God then warned them not to rely upon their adversaries.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God then warned them not to rely upon their adversaries."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":5093,"arabic_characters":3261},{"id":"q3-149-150","q":"3:149–150","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:149–150 · Allegiance under pressure and steadfastness in spiritual detachment","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 418–418","cairo":"I:418","source_page_first":418,"source_page_last":418,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تُطِيعُوا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا يَرُدُّوكُمْ عَلى أَعْقابِكُمْ فَتَنْقَلِبُوا خاسِرِينَ (149) بَلِ اللَّهُ مَوْلاكُمْ وَهُوَ خَيْرُ النَّاصِرِينَ (150)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تُطِيعُوا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وهم المنافقون، لما قالوا للمسلمين عند الهزيمة: ارجعوا إلى دينكم الأول، ولو كان نبيّاً ما قتل، يَرُدُّوكُمْ عَلى أَعْقابِكُمْ راجعين عن إيمانكم، فَتَنْقَلِبُوا خاسِرِينَ مفتونين عن دينكم، فتحبط أعمالكم فتخسروا الدنيا والآخرة، بل اثبتوا على إيمانكم، فإن الله مَوْلاكُمْ سينصركم ويعزكم، وَهُوَ خَيْرُ النَّاصِرِينَ، وقيل: إن تسكنوا إلى أبي سفيان وأشياعه وتستأمنوهم يردوكم إلى دينهم. وقيل: عام في مطاوعة الكفرة والنزول على حكمهم فإنه يجر إلى موافقتهم على دينهم، لا سيما إن طالت مدة الاستئمان.\nقلت: وهذا هو السبب في ارتداد من بقي من المسلمين بالأندلس حتى رجعوا نصارى، هم وأولادهم، والعياذ بالله من سوء القضاء.\nالإشارة: يا أيها المريدون- وخصوصاً المتجردين- إن تطيعوا العامة، وتركنوا إليهم، يردوكم على أعقابكم فتنقلبوا خاسرين بطلب الدنيا وتعاطي أسبابها، فتزلَّ قدمٌ بعد ثُبْوتها، وتنحط من الهمة العالية إلى الهمة السفلى، فإن الطباع تُسرق، والمرء على دين خليله، بل اثبتوا على التجريد وتحقيق التوحيد، فإن الله مولاكم (وهو خير الناصرين) فينصركم ويعزكم ويغنيكم بلا سبب، كما وعدكم وَمَنْ يَتَّقِ اللَّهَ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ مَخْرَجاً وَيَرْزُقْهُ مِنْ حَيْثُ لا يَحْتَسِبُ.\nولّما انصرف أبو سفيان من أحد، قال: بئس ما صنعنا! قتلنا القوم ولم يبق إلا اليسير، ارجعوا حتى نستأصلهم، فألقى الله في قلبه الرعب، كما قال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِنْ تُطِيعُوا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا يَرُدُّوكُمْ عَلى أَعْقابِكُمْ فَتَنْقَلِبُوا خاسِرِينَ (149) بَلِ اللَّهُ مَوْلاكُمْ وَهُوَ خَيْرُ النَّاصِرِينَ (150)","quran_en":"149. O you who believe, if you obey those who disbelieve they will turn you back upon your heels, and you will turn back as losers. 150. Rather, God is your protecting Master, and He is the best of helpers.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Competing identifications of those to be obeyed","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe, if you obey those who disbelieve”—understood here as the hypocrites who, at the time of the defeat, said to Muslims, “Return to your former religion; if he had been a prophet, he would not have been killed”—“they will turn you back upon your heels,” away from your faith, “so you will return as losers,” seduced from your religion so that your works become futile and you lose both abodes. Rather, remain firm in faith: “God is your protecting Master (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مولاكم<\/bdi>)”; He will grant you help and honour, “and He is the best of helpers.”<\/p><p>According to another explanation: if you seek reassurance and protection from Abū Sufyān and his followers and rely upon them, they will bring you back to their religion. Or the wording is general: obeying unbelievers and submitting to their decisions leads to agreement with them in religion, particularly when the dependence extends over a long period. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N142\">[XIV-N142]<\/a><\/p><p>I say: This is the cause of the apostasy of the Muslims who remained in al-Andalus, until they and their children became Christians. We seek refuge in God from an evil decree. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> This reproduces the author’s historical generalization; it is not independently verified here for every surviving Andalusi Muslim or family.<\/span> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N143\">[XIV-N143]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: “O you who believe, if you obey those who disbelieve”—understood here as the hypocrites who, at the time of the defeat, said to Muslims, “Return to your former religion; if he had been a prophet, he would not have been killed”—“they will turn you back upon your heels,” away from your faith, “so you will return as losers,” seduced from your religion so that your works become futile and you lose both abodes. Rather, remain firm in faith: “God is your protecting Master ( مولاكم )”; He will grant you help and honour, “and He is the best of helpers.” According to another explanation: if you seek reassurance and protection from Abū Sufyān and his followers and rely upon them, they will bring you back to their religion. Or the wording is general: obeying unbelievers and submitting to their decisions leads to agreement with them in religion, particularly when the dependence extends over a long period. [XIV-N142] I say: This is the cause of the apostasy of the Muslims who remained in al-Andalus, until they and their children became Christians. We seek refuge in God from an evil decree. Translator’s note: This reproduces the author’s historical generalization; it is not independently verified here for every surviving Andalusi Muslim or family. [XIV-N143]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Steadfastness in tajrīd when the world calls one back","html":"<p>O disciples, especially those who have entered detachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتجردين<\/bdi>): if you obey the common people and rely upon them, they will turn you back upon your heels, and you will become losers through seeking worldly life and occupying yourselves with its means. A foot slips after it had been firmly planted, and high aspiration (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمة العالية<\/bdi>) sinks into the low. Natures borrow from one another (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطباع تُسرق<\/bdi>); a person follows the religion or way of his intimate friend (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المرء على دين خليله<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Rather, remain firm in detachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التجريد<\/bdi>) and in realizing divine unity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تحقيق التوحيد<\/bdi>), for “God is your protecting Master, and He is the best of helpers.” He will help, honour and enrich you without dependence on external means, as He has promised: “Whoever is mindful of God, He grants him a way out and provides for him from where he did not reckon” [Q 65:2–3]. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N144\">[XIV-N144]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"O disciples, especially those who have entered detachment ( المتجردين ): if you obey the common people and rely upon them, they will turn you back upon your heels, and you will become losers through seeking worldly life and occupying yourselves with its means. A foot slips after it had been firmly planted, and high aspiration ( الهمة العالية ) sinks into the low. Natures borrow from one another ( الطباع تُسرق ); a person follows the religion or way of his intimate friend ( المرء على دين خليله ). Rather, remain firm in detachment ( التجريد ) and in realizing divine unity ( تحقيق التوحيد ), for “God is your protecting Master, and He is the best of helpers.” He will help, honour and enrich you without dependence on external means, as He has promised: “Whoever is mindful of God, He grants him a way out and provides for him from where he did not reckon” [Q 65:2–3]. [XIV-N144]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When Abū Sufyān withdrew from Uḥud, he said: “How badly we acted! We killed the men and only a few remain. Let us return and destroy them utterly.” God cast terror into his heart, as the following verse describes.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When Abū Sufyān withdrew from Uḥud, he said: “How badly we acted! We killed the men and only a few remain. Let us return and destroy them utterly.” God cast terror into his heart, as the following verse describes."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2400,"arabic_characters":1461},{"id":"q3-151","q":"3:151","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:151 · Terror, idolatry, and the safety of the spiritual poor","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 419–419","cairo":"I:419","source_page_first":419,"source_page_last":419,"ar":"سَنُلْقِي فِي قُلُوبِ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا الرُّعْبَ بِما أَشْرَكُوا بِاللَّهِ ما لَمْ يُنَزِّلْ بِهِ سُلْطاناً وَمَأْواهُمُ النَّارُ وَبِئْسَ مَثْوَى الظَّالِمِينَ (151)\nقلت: (الرعب) : الخوف، وفيه الضم والسكون، وهكذا كل ثلاثي ساكن الوسط، كالقدس والعسر واليسر، وشبه ذلك، و (بما أشركوا) : مصدرية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: سنقذف فِي قُلُوبِ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا كأبي سفيان وأصحابه، الرُّعْبَ والخوف، حتى يرجعوا عنكم بلا سبب، بسبب شركهم بالله ما لَمْ يُنَزِّلْ بِهِ سُلْطاناً ولا حجة على استحقاق العبادة، وَمَأْواهُمُ النَّارُ أي: هي مقامهم، وَبِئْسَ مَثْوَى الظَّالِمِينَ أي: قبح مقامهم. ووضع الظاهر موضع المضمر للتغليظ في العلة.\nالإشارة: فيها تسلية للفقراء، فإنَّ كل من هم بإذايتهم ألقى الله في قلبه الرعب، حتى لا يقدر أن يتوصل إليهم بشيء مما أمَّل فيهم، وقد رأيتهم هموا بقتلهم وضربهم وحبسهم، وسعوا في ذلك جهدهم، وعملوا في ذلك بينات على زعمهم، تُوجب قتلهم، فكفاهم الله أمرهم، وألقى الرعب في قلوبهم، فانقلبوا خائبين وماتوا ظالمين، والله ولى المتقين.\nثم ذكّرهم الله تعالى ما وعدهم من النصر، فقال:","quran_ar":"سَنُلْقِي فِي قُلُوبِ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا الرُّعْبَ بِما أَشْرَكُوا بِاللَّهِ ما لَمْ يُنَزِّلْ بِهِ سُلْطاناً وَمَأْواهُمُ النَّارُ وَبِئْسَ مَثْوَى الظَّالِمِينَ (151)","quran_en":"151. We shall cast terror into the hearts of those who disbelieve, because they have associated with God that for which He has sent down no authority. Their refuge is the Fire; how evil is the abode of the wrongdoers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The pronunciation of ruʿb and the causal construction","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Ruʿb<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرُّعْب<\/bdi>) is fear; its middle consonant may be pronounced with ḍamma or without a vowel (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السكون<\/bdi>), as with <strong>quds, ʿusr, yusr<\/strong> and other triliteral nouns with a vowelless middle consonant. In <strong>bimā ashrakū<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بِمَا أَشْرَكُوا<\/bdi>), <strong>mā<\/strong> is <strong>maṣdariyya<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدرية<\/bdi>), making the clause equivalent to a verbal noun.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Ruʿb ( الرُّعْب ) is fear; its middle consonant may be pronounced with ḍamma or without a vowel ( السكون ), as with quds, ʿusr, yusr and other triliteral nouns with a vowelless middle consonant. In bimā ashrakū ( بِمَا أَشْرَكُوا ), mā is maṣdariyya ( مصدرية ), making the clause equivalent to a verbal noun."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Fear caused by association without authority","html":"<p>The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: We shall cast terror and fear into the hearts of those who disbelieve, such as Abū Sufyān and his companions, so that they withdraw from you even without some outward cause. This comes because they associate with God that for whose entitlement to worship He has revealed no authority (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سلطان<\/bdi>) or proof (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حجة<\/bdi>). Their refuge and dwelling is the Fire. “How evil is the abode of the wrongdoers!” The explicit noun “wrongdoers” stands in place of the pronoun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إظهار في موضع الإضمار<\/bdi>), making the reason for condemnation more forceful.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, exalted is His majesty, says: We shall cast terror and fear into the hearts of those who disbelieve, such as Abū Sufyān and his companions, so that they withdraw from you even without some outward cause. This comes because they associate with God that for whose entitlement to worship He has revealed no authority ( سلطان ) or proof ( حجة ). Their refuge and dwelling is the Fire. “How evil is the abode of the wrongdoers!” The explicit noun “wrongdoers” stands in place of the pronoun ( إظهار في موضع الإضمار ), making the reason for condemnation more forceful."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"God's protection of the spiritual poor","html":"<p>This offers consolation to the spiritual poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>): whoever intends to injure them finds God casting fear into his heart, so that he cannot accomplish what he hoped against them. I have seen people resolve to kill, beat or imprison them, exerting themselves and fabricating what they considered evidence entailing their execution. God sufficed them against such men, cast fear into their hearts, and they returned disappointed and died as wrongdoers. God is the protecting Friend of the Godfearing.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"This offers consolation to the spiritual poor ( الفقراء ): whoever intends to injure them finds God casting fear into his heart, so that he cannot accomplish what he hoped against them. I have seen people resolve to kill, beat or imprison them, exerting themselves and fabricating what they considered evidence entailing their execution. God sufficed them against such men, cast fear into their hearts, and they returned disappointed and died as wrongdoers. God is the protecting Friend of the Godfearing."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Then He reminded the believers of the victory He had promised them.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Then He reminded the believers of the victory He had promised them."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1459,"arabic_characters":985},{"id":"q3-152","q":"3:152","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:152 · The reversal at Uḥud: obedience, spoils and the conditions of arrival","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 419–421","cairo":"I:419–421","source_page_first":419,"source_page_last":421,"ar":"وَلَقَدْ صَدَقَكُمُ اللَّهُ وَعْدَهُ إِذْ تَحُسُّونَهُمْ بِإِذْنِهِ حَتَّى إِذا فَشِلْتُمْ وَتَنازَعْتُمْ فِي الْأَمْرِ وَعَصَيْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما أَراكُمْ ما تُحِبُّونَ مِنْكُمْ مَنْ يُرِيدُ الدُّنْيا وَمِنْكُمْ مَنْ يُرِيدُ الْآخِرَةَ ثُمَّ صَرَفَكُمْ عَنْهُمْ لِيَبْتَلِيَكُمْ وَلَقَدْ عَفا عَنْكُمْ وَاللَّهُ ذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ (152)\nقلت: حسَّه: إذا قتله وأبطل حسه، وجواب (إذا) : محذوف، أي: حتى إذا فشلتم وتنازعتم وعصيتم امتحناكم بالهزيمة، والواو لا ترتب، والتقدير: حتى إذا تنازعتم وعصيتم وفشلتم سلبنا النصر عنكم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَقَدْ صَدَقَكُمُ اللَّهُ ما وعدكم من النصر لو صبرتم واتقيتم، وذلك حين كنتم تَحُسُّونَهُمْ بالسيف، وتقتلونهم حتى انهزموا هاربين، بإذنه تعالى وإرادته، حَتَّى إِذا فَشِلْتُمْ أي: جبنتم\nوضعف رأيكم وملتم إلى الغنيمة، وَتَنازَعْتُمْ في الثبات مع الرماة حين انهزم المشركون، فقلتم: الغنيمةَ الغنيمةَ، فما وقوفكم هنا! وقال آخرون: لا تخالفوا أمر الرسول، ثم تركتم المركز، وَعَصَيْتُمْ الرسول مِنْ بَعْدِ ما أَراكُمْ ما تُحِبُّونَ من النصر والغنيمة، امتحناكم حينئذ بالهزيمة.\nفمنكم مَنْ يُرِيدُ الدُّنْيا ليصرفها في الآخرة، وهم الذين خالفوا المركز وذهبوا للغنيمة، وَمِنْكُمْ مَنْ يُرِيدُ الْآخِرَةَ صِرفاً، وهم الثابتون مع عبد الله بن جبير، محافظةً على أمر رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، ثُمَّ صَرَفَكُمْ عَنْهُمْ حين خالفتم أمر الرسول، لِيَبْتَلِيَكُمْ أي: ليختبركم، فيتبين الصابر من الجازع، والمخلص من المنافق، وَلَقَدْ عَفا عَنْكُمْ فلم يستأصلكم بعد المعصية والمخالفة، لاستحقاقكم ذلك، أو تجاوز عن ذنبكم وتفضل بالتوبة والمغفرة، وَاللَّهُ ذُو فَضْلٍ عظيم عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ يتفضل عليهم بالمغفرة في الأحوال كلها، سواء أديل عليهم أو لهم، فإن الابتلاء أيضاً رحمة وتطهير. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: يقول للفقراء الذين استشرفوا على بلاد الخصوصية، ثم فشلوا ورجعوا إلى بلاد العمومية: ولقد صدقكم الله وعده في إدراك الخصوصية لو صبرتم، فإنكم حين كنتم تجاهدون نفوسكم وتحسونها بسيوف المخالفة، لمعت لكم أنوار المشاهدة، حتى إذا فشلتم وتفرقت قلوبكم، وعصيتم شيوخكم قلَّت أمدادكم، وأظلمت قلوبكم، من بعد ما رأيتم ما تحبون من مبادئ المشاهدة، فملتم إلى الدنيا الفانية، فمنكم يا معشر المنتسبين من يريد الدنيا، فصحب العارفين على حرف، وهو الذي رجع وفشل، ومنكم من يريد الآخرة وقطع يأسه من الرجوع إلى الدنيا، وهو الذي ثبت حتى ظفر، ثم صرفكم عن صحبة العارفين، يا من أراد الدنيا من المنتسبين، ليبتليكم، هل صحبتموهم لله أو لغيره، ولقد عفا عنكم وجعلكم من عوام المسلمين، ولم يسلب عنكم الإيمان عقوبة لترك صحبة العارفين. أو لقد عفا عنكم إن رجعتم إلى صحبتهم والأدب معهم، فإن الله (ذو فضل على المؤمنين) حيث لم يعاجلهم بالعقوبة. وبالله التوفيق.\nوقال الورتجبي: قوله: مِنْكُمْ مَنْ يُرِيدُ الدُّنْيا، أي: منكم من وقع في بحر غنى القدم، واتصف به بنعت التمكين ورؤية النعم في شكر المنعم، كسليمان عليه السلام. ومنكم من وقع في بحر التنزيه وتقديس الأزلية، فغلب عليه القدس والطهارة، فخرج بنعت الفقر تجريداً لتوحيده وإفرادِ قدمه من الحدث، كمحمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم حيث قال: «الفقر فخري(1)» .\nثم بيّن وقت الذلة التي افتقرت إلى العفو، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَقَدْ صَدَقَكُمُ اللَّهُ وَعْدَهُ إِذْ تَحُسُّونَهُمْ بِإِذْنِهِ حَتَّى إِذا فَشِلْتُمْ وَتَنازَعْتُمْ فِي الْأَمْرِ وَعَصَيْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما أَراكُمْ ما تُحِبُّونَ مِنْكُمْ مَنْ يُرِيدُ الدُّنْيا وَمِنْكُمْ مَنْ يُرِيدُ الْآخِرَةَ ثُمَّ صَرَفَكُمْ عَنْهُمْ لِيَبْتَلِيَكُمْ وَلَقَدْ عَفا عَنْكُمْ وَاللَّهُ ذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ (152)","quran_en":"152. God certainly fulfilled His promise to you when, by His leave, you were cutting them down, until you lost courage, disputed over the command and disobeyed, after He had shown you what you loved. Some of you desired this world and some desired the Hereafter. Then He turned you away from them to try you. Yet He has pardoned you, and God is full of bounty toward the believers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Ḥassa (حسّ), the suppressed answer and wāw","html":"<p>I say: To <strong>ḥassa<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حسَّ<\/bdi>) someone is to kill him, abolishing his sensation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حِسّ<\/bdi>). The answer (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جواب<\/bdi>) of <strong>idhā<\/strong> is omitted (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">محذوف<\/bdi>): “until, when you lost courage, disputed and disobeyed, We tried you by defeat.” The conjunction <strong>wāw<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواو<\/bdi>) does not itself specify chronological order. The sequence of events is understood as: when you disputed, disobeyed and lost courage, We withdrew victory from you.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: To ḥassa ( حسَّ ) someone is to kill him, abolishing his sensation ( حِسّ ). The answer ( جواب ) of idhā is omitted ( محذوف ): “until, when you lost courage, disputed and disobeyed, We tried you by defeat.” The conjunction wāw ( الواو ) does not itself specify chronological order. The sequence of events is understood as: when you disputed, disobeyed and lost courage, We withdrew victory from you."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The archers, the booty and divine pardon","html":"<p>God proved truthful in His promise of victory if you remained patient and mindful of Him: you were striking down your enemies with swords, killing and routing them by His leave and will. But then your resolution weakened, your judgment faltered and you inclined towards the spoils. The archers disputed whether to stay at their station after the idolaters fled. Some cried, “The spoils! The spoils! Why remain here?” Others replied, “Do not violate the Messenger's command.” Then you abandoned your station and disobeyed the Messenger, after seeing the triumph and spoils you loved, and at that point He tried you with defeat.<\/p><p>Among you were those seeking the world <i>in order to employ it for the Hereafter<\/i>—those who left their post for the spoils. Among you were others who desired the Hereafter without admixture: those who remained with ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr in fidelity to the Messenger's command. God then turned you away from the enemy because you violated that command, to try you and disclose the patient and the distressed, the sincere and the hypocrite. Yet He pardoned you: He did not annihilate you after your disobedience and opposition, although you merited punishment; or He forgave your offence and favoured you with repentance and mercy. His bounty towards believers is immense: He favours them with forgiveness in every circumstance, whether fortune alternates against or in their favour; trial itself is mercy and purification. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God proved truthful in His promise of victory if you remained patient and mindful of Him: you were striking down your enemies with swords, killing and routing them by His leave and will. But then your resolution weakened, your judgment faltered and you inclined towards the spoils. The archers disputed whether to stay at their station after the idolaters fled. Some cried, “The spoils! The spoils! Why remain here?” Others replied, “Do not violate the Messenger's command.” Then you abandoned your station and disobeyed the Messenger, after seeing the triumph and spoils you loved, and at that point He tried you with defeat. Among you were those seeking the world in order to employ it for the Hereafter —those who left their post for the spoils. Among you were others who desired the Hereafter without admixture: those who remained with ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr in fidelity to the Messenger's command. God then turned you away from the enemy because you violated that command, to try you and disclose the patient and the distressed, the sincere and the hypocrite. Yet He pardoned you: He did not annihilate you after your disobedience and opposition, although you merited punishment; or He forgave your offence and favoured you with repentance and mercy. His bounty towards believers is immense: He favours them with forgiveness in every circumstance, whether fortune alternates against or in their favour; trial itself is mercy and purification. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Leaving the country of particular realization for ordinary life","html":"<p>The Real addresses the spiritual poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>) who had approached the country of particular realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بلاد الخصوصية<\/bdi>) but then weakened and returned to ordinary existence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بلاد العمومية<\/bdi>): God fulfilled His promise that you would attain the rank of the elect, if only you had persevered. While you struggled against the lower selves, cutting them down with the swords of contravention (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سيوف المخالفة<\/bdi>), the lights of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنوار المشاهدة<\/bdi>) flashed before you. But when you weakened, your hearts scattered, and you disobeyed your spiritual teachers, the reinforcements granted you diminished and your hearts darkened, after you had seen the beginnings of witnessing. You inclined to the vanishing world.<\/p><p>Among those affiliated to the path (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المنتسبين<\/bdi>) is one who desires this world and accompanies realized masters only precariously, “on an edge”; he is the one who turns back. Among them is one who seeks the Hereafter and relinquishes hope of returning to worldly preoccupation; he remains until he obtains the goal. God then diverts those still seeking the world away from the company of realized masters to test whether they accompanied them for God or another motive. Yet He has pardoned you and made you ordinary Muslims, without stripping you of faith as a penalty for abandoning the realized masters. Or His forgiveness is yours when you return to their company and observe good manners (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأدب<\/bdi>). God is generous towards believers and does not hasten their punishment. And success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real addresses the spiritual poor ( الفقراء ) who had approached the country of particular realization ( بلاد الخصوصية ) but then weakened and returned to ordinary existence ( بلاد العمومية ): God fulfilled His promise that you would attain the rank of the elect, if only you had persevered. While you struggled against the lower selves, cutting them down with the swords of contravention ( سيوف المخالفة ), the lights of witnessing ( أنوار المشاهدة ) flashed before you. But when you weakened, your hearts scattered, and you disobeyed your spiritual teachers, the reinforcements granted you diminished and your hearts darkened, after you had seen the beginnings of witnessing. You inclined to the vanishing world. Among those affiliated to the path ( المنتسبين ) is one who desires this world and accompanies realized masters only precariously, “on an edge”; he is the one who turns back. Among them is one who seeks the Hereafter and relinquishes hope of returning to worldly preoccupation; he remains until he obtains the goal. God then diverts those still seeking the world away from the company of realized masters to test whether they accompanied them for God or another motive. Yet He has pardoned you and made you ordinary Muslims, without stripping you of faith as a penalty for abandoning the realized masters. Or His forgiveness is yours when you return to their company and observe good manners ( الأدب ). God is generous towards believers and does not hasten their punishment. And success is through God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Rūzbihān al-Baqlī on possession and transcendence","html":"<p>Al-Wartajbī [Rūzbihān] interprets “Some of you desire this world” as referring to those immersed in the ocean of pre-eternal richness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غنى القدم<\/bdi>), established in it and seeing blessings with gratitude to their Giver, like Solomon. “And some of you desire the Hereafter” refers to those plunged in the ocean of transcendence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التنزيه<\/bdi>) and the sanctification of pre-eternity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تقديس الأزلية<\/bdi>), so overtaken by holiness and purity that they emerged in the guise of poverty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقر<\/bdi>) and detachment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التجريد<\/bdi>), affirming the unique pre-eternity of God apart from contingent being (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحدث<\/bdi>), like Muḥammad, upon him blessings and peace, who is here quoted as saying, “Poverty is my pride<a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/CP19\" title=\"Cairo print critical note CP19\">[CP19]<\/a>.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Al-Wartajbī [Rūzbihān] interprets “Some of you desire this world” as referring to those immersed in the ocean of pre-eternal richness ( غنى القدم ), established in it and seeing blessings with gratitude to their Giver, like Solomon. “And some of you desire the Hereafter” refers to those plunged in the ocean of transcendence ( التنزيه ) and the sanctification of pre-eternity ( تقديس الأزلية ), so overtaken by holiness and purity that they emerged in the guise of poverty ( الفقر ) and detachment ( التجريد ), affirming the unique pre-eternity of God apart from contingent being ( الحدث ), like Muḥammad, upon him blessings and peace, who is here quoted as saying, “Poverty is my pride [CP19] .”"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then specified the time of humiliation that required forgiveness.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then specified the time of humiliation that required forgiveness."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C420.1","page":420,"ar":"قال الحافظ ابن حجر: لا أصل له. انظر: الأسرار المرفوعة.","en":"The ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar said: “It has no foundation.” See al-Asrār al-marfūʿa.","anchor":"الفقر فخري"}],"english_characters":4154,"arabic_characters":2836},{"id":"q3-153","q":"3:153","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:153 · Flight from the Prophet and flight from the teachers","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 421–422","cairo":"I:421–422","source_page_first":421,"source_page_last":422,"ar":"إِذْ تُصْعِدُونَ وَلا تَلْوُونَ عَلى أَحَدٍ وَالرَّسُولُ يَدْعُوكُمْ فِي أُخْراكُمْ فَأَثابَكُمْ غَمًّا بِغَمٍّ لِكَيْلا تَحْزَنُوا عَلى ما فاتَكُمْ وَلا ما أَصابَكُمْ وَاللَّهُ خَبِيرٌ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ (153)\nقلت: (إذْ) : ظرف لعفا، أو اذكر. وأصعد: أبعد في الأرض، وصعد: في الجبل، فالإصعاد: الذهاب في الأرض المستوية، والصعود: الارتقاء في العلو. وقرئ بهما معاً لأنهما وقعا معاً، فمنهم من فرّ ذاهباً في الأرض، ومنهم من صعد إلى الجبل. و (لكيلا) : متعلق بأثابكم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولقد عفا عنكم حين كنتم تُصْعِدُونَ عن نبيه- عليه الصلاة والسلام-، منهزمين عنه، تبعدون عنه، وَلا تَلْوُونَ عَلى أَحَدٍ أي: لا يلتفت بعضكم إلى بعض، ولا ينتظر بعضكم بعضاً، وَالرَّسُولُ محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم يَدْعُوكُمْ فِي أُخْراكُمْ أي: في ساقتكم، يقول: «إليَّ عباد الله، أنا رسول الله، من يكرُّ فله الجنة» ، وفيه مدح للرسول صلّى الله عليه وسلم بالشجاعة والثبات، حيث وقف في آخر المنهزمين، فإن الآخر هو موقف الأبطال، والفرار فى حقه صلّى الله عليه وسلم محال.\nفَأَثابَكُمْ أي: فجازاكم على ذلك الفرار، غَمًّا وهو ظهور المشركين عليكم وقتل إخوانكم، بسبب غم أوصلتموه للنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلم بعصيانه والفرار عنه، وقدَّر ذلك لِكَيْلا تَحْزَنُوا عَلى ما فاتَكُمْ من الغنيمة، وَلا على ما أَصابَكُمْ من الجرح والهزيمة، لأن من استحق العقوبة والأدب لا يحزن على ما فاته ولا على ما أصابه إذ جريمته تستحق أكثر من ذلك، يرى ما نزل به بعض ما يستحقه، فيهون عليه أمر ما نزل به أو ما فاته من الخير.\nأو يقول: فَأَثابَكُمْ غَمًّا متصلاً بِغَمٍّ فالغم الأول: ما فاتهم من الظفر والغنيمة، والثاني: ما نالهم من القتل والهزيمة، أو الأول: ما أصابهم من القتل والجراح، والثاني: ما سمعوا من الإرجاف بقتل النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، وذلك ليتمرنوا على المحن والشدائد حتى لا يجزعوا من شيء. وبذلك وصفهم كعب بن زهير في لاميته، حيث قال:\nلاَ يَفْرَحُونَ إِذا نَالَتْ رِماحُهُمْ ... وَلَيْسُوا مجازيعاً إذا نِيلُوا\nفإن المتمرّن على المصائب المتعود عليها يهون عليه أمرها، فلا يحزن على ما أصابه ولا ما فاته، وَاللَّهُ خَبِيرٌ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ وبما قصدتم، فيجازيكم على ذلك.\nالإشارة: ما زال الدعاة إلى الله من أهل التربية النبوية يدعون الناس إلى الله، ويعرفونهم بالطريق إلى الله، يبينون لهم الطريق إلى عين التحقيق، والناس يبعدون عنهم ويفرّون منهم، وهم في أخراهم يقولون بلسان الحال أو المقال: يا عباد الله، هلم إلينا نعرفُكم بالله، وندلكم على الله، فلا يلوي إليهم أحد ولا يلتفت إليهم بشر، إلا مَن سبقت له العناية، وأراد الحق تعالى أن يوصله إلى درجة الولاية، «سبحان مَن لم يجعل الدليل على أوليائه إلا من حيث الدليل عليه، ولم يوصل إليهم إلا مَن أراد أن يوصله إليه» ، فأثابهم على الفرار غم الحجاب، متصلاً بغم الأسباب، فلا يحزنوا على ما فاتهم من المعرفة إذ لم يعرفوا قدرها، ولا على ما أصابهم من الغفلة والبطالة، إذ لم يتفطنوا لها، (والله خبير بما تعملون) يا معشر العباد، من التودد أو العناد. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم أنزل على أهل أحد الأمن والطمأنينة بعد الشدة والمحنة، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق، بقوله:","quran_ar":"إِذْ تُصْعِدُونَ وَلا تَلْوُونَ عَلى أَحَدٍ وَالرَّسُولُ يَدْعُوكُمْ فِي أُخْراكُمْ فَأَثابَكُمْ غَمًّا بِغَمٍّ لِكَيْلا تَحْزَنُوا عَلى ما فاتَكُمْ وَلا ما أَصابَكُمْ وَاللَّهُ خَبِيرٌ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ (153)","quran_en":"153. When you were fleeing far across the land, looking back at no one, while the Messenger called you from your rear, He repaid you with grief upon grief, so that you would not grieve over what escaped you or over what befell you. God is fully aware of what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Iṣʿād (إصعاد) and ṣuʿūd (صعود)","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Idh<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ<\/bdi>) is a temporal adverb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظرف<\/bdi>) related to “He pardoned” or to an understood imperative “remember.” <strong>Aṣʿada<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أصعد<\/bdi>) means to go far across the land; <strong>ṣaʿida<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صعد<\/bdi>) means to ascend a mountain. Accordingly <strong>iṣʿād<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإصعاد<\/bdi>) is departing over level ground, and <strong>ṣuʿūd<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصعود<\/bdi>) is rising to higher ground. Both readings have been transmitted, for both occurred: some fled over the land and others climbed the mountain. <strong>Li-kay-lā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لكيلا<\/bdi>) is attached to <strong>athābakum<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أثابكم<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Idh ( إذ ) is a temporal adverb ( ظرف ) related to “He pardoned” or to an understood imperative “remember.” Aṣʿada ( أصعد ) means to go far across the land; ṣaʿida ( صعد ) means to ascend a mountain. Accordingly iṣʿād ( الإصعاد ) is departing over level ground, and ṣuʿūd ( الصعود ) is rising to higher ground. Both readings have been transmitted, for both occurred: some fled over the land and others climbed the mountain. Li-kay-lā ( لكيلا ) is attached to athābakum ( أثابكم )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The griefs of Uḥud and the Messenger's constancy","html":"<p>God pardoned you as you fled the Prophet, moving away in defeat, without one of you turning toward or waiting for another. Yet Muḥammad, the Messenger, was calling to you from your rear guard: “To me, servants of God! I am God's Messenger! Paradise to whoever returns to the fight!” The account praises his courage and steadfastness: he stood at the rear of the retreating men, the station of heroes; flight is inconceivable in his case.<\/p><p>God “rewarded” you for flight with grief: the enemy gained superiority over you and your brothers were killed, because you caused grief to the Prophet through disobedience and flight. He ordained that this should befall you lest you grieve over missed booty or over wounds and defeat. One who understands that an offence deserves correction does not lament what he has lost or suffered, perceiving the affliction as less than his wrongdoing merited, and his burden becomes easier.<\/p><p>Alternatively, He gave you grief joined to grief: first the lost victory and booty, then death and defeat; or first the deaths and wounds, then the rumours that the Prophet had been killed. It was to accustom you to trials and hardship so that you would not panic when struck. Kaʿb ibn Zuhayr described such people in his <i>lāmiyya<\/i>:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">They do not exult when their spears strike home,<br/>nor do they panic when they themselves are struck.<\/blockquote><p>One seasoned by affliction bears it lightly and is not undone by what he suffers or loses. God knows your deeds and intentions and recompenses you accordingly.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God pardoned you as you fled the Prophet, moving away in defeat, without one of you turning toward or waiting for another. Yet Muḥammad, the Messenger, was calling to you from your rear guard: “To me, servants of God! I am God's Messenger! Paradise to whoever returns to the fight!” The account praises his courage and steadfastness: he stood at the rear of the retreating men, the station of heroes; flight is inconceivable in his case. God “rewarded” you for flight with grief: the enemy gained superiority over you and your brothers were killed, because you caused grief to the Prophet through disobedience and flight. He ordained that this should befall you lest you grieve over missed booty or over wounds and defeat. One who understands that an offence deserves correction does not lament what he has lost or suffered, perceiving the affliction as less than his wrongdoing merited, and his burden becomes easier. Alternatively, He gave you grief joined to grief: first the lost victory and booty, then death and defeat; or first the deaths and wounds, then the rumours that the Prophet had been killed. It was to accustom you to trials and hardship so that you would not panic when struck. Kaʿb ibn Zuhayr described such people in his lāmiyya : They do not exult when their spears strike home, nor do they panic when they themselves are struck. One seasoned by affliction bears it lightly and is not undone by what he suffers or loses. God knows your deeds and intentions and recompenses you accordingly."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The call of the spiritual guides and the grief of veiling","html":"<p>Inviters to God, bearing the inheritance of Prophetic spiritual education (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل التربية النبوية<\/bdi>), have never ceased calling people to God, teaching the way to Him and pointing them towards the very reality of realization (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين التحقيق<\/bdi>). People nevertheless distance themselves and flee. The guides call behind them, by speech or by their state: “Servants of God, come to us; we shall make God known to you and guide you to Him.” No one turns to them or looks towards them except those preceded by divine solicitude (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العناية<\/bdi>), whom the Real intends to bring to sainthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية<\/bdi>). As the saying has it: “Glory be to Him who provided no proof of His friends save what is itself proof of Him, and who brought none to them except one He wished to bring to Himself.”<\/p><p>For fleeing them, people receive the grief of being veiled (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غم الحجاب<\/bdi>), added to the grief of worldly causes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غم الأسباب<\/bdi>). They do not grieve over the gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/bdi>) they missed, because they never recognized its worth, or over the heedlessness and idleness that befall them, because they do not notice them. God knows, O servants, whether your deeds proceed from affection or obstinacy. Success comes from God, the guide to the straight path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Inviters to God, bearing the inheritance of Prophetic spiritual education ( أهل التربية النبوية ), have never ceased calling people to God, teaching the way to Him and pointing them towards the very reality of realization ( عين التحقيق ). People nevertheless distance themselves and flee. The guides call behind them, by speech or by their state: “Servants of God, come to us; we shall make God known to you and guide you to Him.” No one turns to them or looks towards them except those preceded by divine solicitude ( العناية ), whom the Real intends to bring to sainthood ( الولاية ). As the saying has it: “Glory be to Him who provided no proof of His friends save what is itself proof of Him, and who brought none to them except one He wished to bring to Himself.” For fleeing them, people receive the grief of being veiled ( غم الحجاب ), added to the grief of worldly causes ( غم الأسباب ). They do not grieve over the gnosis ( المعرفة ) they missed, because they never recognized its worth, or over the heedlessness and idleness that befall them, because they do not notice them. God knows, O servants, whether your deeds proceed from affection or obstinacy. Success comes from God, the guide to the straight path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>God then sent security upon the people of Uḥud after their difficulty and ordeal.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God then sent security upon the people of Uḥud after their difficulty and ordeal."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3298,"arabic_characters":2749}];
